(window.LING406_STUDY=window.LING406_STUDY||{})["m2"]={"revision":"ling406-study-1-m2","meta":{"module":"m2","exam":"Midterm 2","range":"Speech perception, sentence processing, aphasias, language development, critical periods, second language learning","lead":"These pages cover Module II in the order it was taught, from categorical perception to second language learning. Every question on the Midterm II review deck is answered, and each experiment is tied to its researchers and year.","flags":["There is no answer key for LING406, so every answer here was written from the course decks and their reviewed notes.","Second Language Learning page 21 says the concept for matches partly activates the Russian word for matches. Midterm II review page 54 says the concept for speaker does. In the accepted account, the English word speaker partly activates the Russian word spichki through shared beginning sounds. It does not do so through a concept. The strong conceptual link of spichki then activates the concept of matches. The study pages follow the page 54 wording.","The hemispherectomy page says children whose left hemisphere is removed seem to develop typical language abilities. Later studies usually find subtle language weaknesses after early left hemispherectomy, which fits the next page on perinatal stroke and its low-normal outcomes.","The Ellis (2005) page says untimed GJT performance is strongly correlated with years of formal education in L2. Table 14 on that page gives a correlation of .27 for untimed GJT ungrammatical items and .03 for grammatical items. The .27 value is significant but modest, so strongly overstates it."]},"topics":[{"id":"m2-speech-signal","title":"From a messy signal to speech sounds","tier":"Possible","blocks":[{"refs":["speech1-02","speech1-03"],"main":"**Listeners pull one stable message out of a speech signal that is continuous and varies from speaker to speaker.** **Speech perception** is the process of recovering a linguistic message from the sound of speech. In the spectrogram of “I can see you,” each sound runs into the next, so nothing marks where letters or words begin. The tongue and lips move without pause, so the movement for one sound overlaps the next.","sub":["Ten vowels from 76 speakers were plotted by their first and second **formants** (frequency regions with strong acoustic energy). Each vowel covered a broad oval, and many of the ovals overlapped.","So one pair of formant values can be one vowel for one speaker and a different vowel for another speaker."],"end":"So the listener has to turn a highly variable **analog signal** (one that changes continuously) into something more abstract. **Categorical perception** is thought to be that translation from physical to symbolic."},{"refs":["speech1-04","speech1-05","speech1-06","speech1-07"],"main":"**Formants are the resonant frequencies the vocal tract amplifies, and their pattern tells vowels apart.** The shape of the **vocal tract** decides which frequencies get boosted. Moving the tongue, jaw and lips changes that shape, so it changes the formants. **F1** is the lowest formant and **F2** is the next one up.","sub":["In bead, F1 is very low and F2 is very high, so the two sit far apart. In booed, F1 and F2 are both low.","A recorded /u/ had F1 near 300 Hz and a very low F2 near 900 Hz. Praat found five formants, but only the first three usually matter for phonemes."],"end":"So a vowel’s identity lies mostly in the relationship between its first two formants."},{"refs":["speech1-08","speech1-09","speech1-10"],"main":"**Source-filter synthesis separates the voice from the vocal tract, so a vowel can be changed by swapping only the filter.** The **source** is the sound energy the vocal folds make. The **filter** is how the vocal tract shapes that energy. Praat estimated the filter from the formants and removed it, and the leftover source sounded like a buzz.","sub":["Three filters were applied to that one source. The first kept the original formants, the second raised F1 a little, and the third raised F2 a lot.","Multiplying the source by each filter gave three different vowels with the same buzz and pitch."],"end":"So vowel quality comes from the filter, and new vowels can be made without recording a new speaker."},{"refs":["speech1-11","speech1-12"],"main":"**Formant values change with the speaker, the speaking rate and the neighboring sounds, but they still describe vowel space well for one careful talker.** Vocal tracts differ in size and shape, so their resonances differ. Fast speech gives the articulators less time to reach their targets. Neighboring sounds pull the tongue and lips toward them.","sub":["**Vowel space** is the layout of a language’s vowels by their formant frequencies. Reasonable vowels can be made by setting the **steady-state formants** (the stable middle of a vowel).","The **pattern playback synthesizer** turned painted spectrogram patterns back into sound. Researchers could change one painted formant and keep everything else the same."],"end":"So controlled synthetic speech made it possible to test exactly which acoustic change listeners respond to."}],"questions":[{"q":"Why is speech perception a hard problem even though listeners do it easily?","a":"Speech perception is hard because the signal is continuous and highly variable. The sounds of “I can see you” run together with no gaps between words, because the articulators move without pause. The same vowel also has different formant values for different speakers, so the listener must find one stable message inside a lot of physical variation.","refs":["speech1-02","speech1-03"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"Ten vowels from 76 speakers were plotted by their first and second formants. What did the plot reveal?","a":"Each vowel covered a broad region instead of a single point, and many regions overlapped. This is because speakers differ in anatomy, speaking rate and context, so the same vowel has different formant values from different people. So one pair of formant values can be one vowel for one speaker and another vowel for a different speaker.","refs":["speech1-02","speech1-11"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"In source-filter synthesis, what happened when only the filter was changed?","a":"Changing only the filter turned /u/ into different vowels while the buzz and pitch stayed the same. This is because the source supplies the sound energy and the filter decides which frequencies stay strong. So vowel identity lies in the filter, which is the formant pattern of the vocal tract.","refs":["speech1-08","speech1-09","speech1-10"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"What was the pattern playback synthesizer, and why was it useful?","a":"The pattern playback synthesizer turned painted spectrogram patterns into sound. Researchers could change a single formant or transition and keep everything else the same. So any change in what listeners heard could be traced to that one painted change.","refs":["speech1-12","speech1-13"],"tier":"Possible"}]},{"id":"m2-categorical-perception","title":"Categorical perception on Liberman’s continuum","tier":"On the review deck","blocks":[{"refs":["speech1-13","speech1-14","review-2"],"main":"**Stop consonants such as /b/, /d/ and /g/ are told apart by their formant transitions.** A **formant transition** is a change in a formant’s frequency over time. The audible part of a stop happens while the jaw is opening, so the formants glide from the consonant toward the vowel. **Liberman and colleagues (1957)** used the pattern playback synthesizer to copy these transitions.","sub":["The **speech continuum** kept F1 constant and varied the **F2 onset frequency** (where the second formant starts) in equal steps from stimulus 1 to 13.","So adjacent stimuli differed by the same amount across the entire continuum. A rising F2 was heard as /b/, a flat one as /d/ and a falling one as /g/."],"end":"So any difference in how pairs are heard cannot come from the size of the physical step, because every step is equal."},{"refs":["speech1-15","speech1-16","review-3","review-4"],"main":"**A non-categorical ideal observer predicts gradual labeling and flat discrimination.** An **ideal observer model** predicts how a listener would respond if perception worked a particular way. Here perception is assumed to track the acoustics linearly. Then labeling would get “blurry” as stimuli move away from each **prototype** (the clearest example of a category).","sub":["Stimulus 2 would be heard as 75 percent /b/ and 25 percent /d/, because it sits a quarter of the way from /b/ toward /d/.","**Labeling** has to be peaked because there are three categories. But **discrimination** only asks “same” or “different,” so it should be independent of labeling. If F2 differences were perceived equally everywhere, discrimination accuracy would be constant."],"end":"So a flat discrimination line is what non-categorical perception predicts."},{"refs":["speech1-17","review-5"],"main":"**Real listeners labeled sharply and discriminated best at the category boundaries.** In **Liberman and colleagues (1957)**, labels switched from /b/ to /d/ and from /d/ to /g/ over just a step or two. Discrimination **peaked** at those two boundaries. Pairs inside one category were told apart barely above chance.","sub":["The **category boundary** is the narrow region where the label flips.","Discrimination predicted from the labels captured much of the real pattern. Real accuracy was a little higher, so listeners keep some detail within a category."],"end":"So equal physical steps sound unequal, because similarity is defined by category rather than by acoustics. This pattern is **categorical perception**."},{"refs":["speech1-19","speech1-20","speech1-21"],"main":"**Class data on a beach to peach continuum gave the same pattern, with each listener’s best discrimination near that listener’s own boundary.** Identification stayed on one word and then switched near the middle. Not everyone had exactly the same boundary. But discrimination was best for stimuli heard as different categories.","sub":["The discrimination test used only “different” trials, so “different” was always the correct answer.","Some class data were noisy. With few trials and no “same” trials, a single response could move accuracy a lot."],"end":"So the core finding held even in a short, imperfect class experiment."},{"refs":["speech1-22"],"main":"**Being worse at hearing differences inside a category helps, because it lets many physically different tokens count as one phoneme.** Every production of a phoneme differs because of speakers, speaking rates and neighboring sounds. If every small change were heard as a new sound, one word would sound like many unrelated sounds.","end":"So categorical perception keeps the differences that change words and ignores the ones that do not."}],"questions":[{"q":"What is categorical perception?","a":"Categorical perception means that equal acoustic differences are hard to hear inside a category and easy to hear across a category boundary. Listeners label a continuum with sharp switches, and their discrimination peaks at those switches. So similarity between speech sounds is defined by categories rather than by physical distance.","refs":["speech1-17","speech1-03"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"Why did Liberman and colleagues vary F2 onset in equal steps while keeping F1 constant?","a":"They did it so that adjacent stimuli differ by the same physical amount everywhere on the continuum. Holding F1 constant removes any other difference between stimuli. So if some pairs are easier to tell apart, the advantage must come from the category boundary and not from the acoustics.","refs":["speech1-14","review-2"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"What would discrimination look like if perception were not categorical, and what did Liberman and colleagues (1957) find instead?","a":"If perception were not categorical, discrimination would be flat, with every equally spaced pair equally easy. Liberman and colleagues found peaks instead. Pairs that crossed the /b/ to /d/ or /d/ to /g/ boundary were discriminated well, and pairs inside a category were near chance.","refs":["speech1-16","speech1-17","review-4","review-5"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"What is the advantage of being worse at hearing some differences between sounds?","a":"The advantage is stable word recognition. Every token of a phoneme differs physically because of speakers, speaking rates and neighboring sounds. Being less sensitive inside a category lets all those tokens count as the same phoneme, while differences that change words stay easy to hear.","refs":["speech1-22"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"In the class beach and peach data, why did the discrimination peaks fall in different places for different listeners?","a":"The peaks fell in different places because not everyone has exactly the same category boundary. Each listener discriminated best near that listener’s own identification boundary. So in general discrimination is best for stimuli heard as different categories.","refs":["speech1-19","speech1-20"],"tier":"Possible"}]},{"id":"m2-infant-speech","title":"Infant categories, Eimas, and perceptual narrowing","tier":"On the review deck","blocks":[{"refs":["development1-02","development1-03"],"main":"**Nativist positions say children have innate knowledge of what language is like, and categorical perception seemed to support them.** **Chomsky** criticized **Skinner** because language is **generative** (it can produce endless new sentences). Acquiring a language means identifying the abstract grammar that produces its sentences. The learning mechanisms studied by behaviorists were not up to that task.","sub":["Grammatical and ungrammatical sentences are not similar in any straightforward way. Children are also not dependably rewarded or punished for grammar, so learning language does not look like **operant conditioning** (learning from rewards and punishments).","Nativists propose **Universal Grammar** (innate knowledge of what language is like) and acquisition processes specific to language."],"end":"Early theories treated categorical perception as specific to language and to humans, so it seemed to fit this nativist view."},{"refs":["speech1-24","development1-04","review-32"],"main":"**Infant speech perception is measured with behaviors that change when an infant notices something new.** In the **conditioned head turn procedure**, the infant learns to turn toward a sound when it changes, and a correct turn earns a visual reward. In **high amplitude sucking**, strong sucks on a pacifier make a sound play.","sub":["As the same sound repeats, the infant gets used to it, which is **habituation**, and sucking drops.","If sucking rises again after the sound changes, the infant noticed the change."],"end":"So researchers can test which sound differences infants hear long before infants can talk."},{"refs":["speech1-25","development1-05","development1-06","review-33","review-34"],"main":"**Eimas and colleagues (1971) found that four-month-olds hear voice onset time categorically.** **Voice onset time** (VOT) is the delay between releasing a stop consonant and the start of voicing. Infants heard one syllable until their sucking dropped. Then the sound changed or stayed the same.","sub":["In the 20D condition, VOT changed by 20 ms across an adult category boundary, from +20 to +40 ms. Sucking recovered sharply.","In the 20S condition, VOT also changed by 20 ms but stayed inside one category. Sucking kept falling, much like the no-change control."],"end":"This was a big deal because four-month-olds are very young and are only starting to make vowel-like sounds. So an apparently language-specific ability this early seemed like evidence for innateness."},{"refs":["speech1-26","development1-07","review-35"],"main":"**Werker and Tees (1984) found that English-learning infants lose the ability to hear non-English contrasts by the end of the first year.** They tested a Hindi Ta/ta contrast and a Salish ki/qi contrast. Most English-learning infants discriminated both at 6 to 8 months. Fewer did at 8 to 10 months, and very few did at 10 to 12 months.","sub":["Hindi-learning and Salish-learning infants kept discriminating their own contrast.","So the decline depends on language experience. It is called **perceptual narrowing**."],"end":"Perceptual narrowing relates to categorical perception because a non-native difference that falls inside one native category comes to be heard as a within-category difference."},{"refs":["speech1-27","development1-08","review-36"],"main":"**These findings built a compelling narrative in which infants start as “citizens of the world” and then narrow to their own language.** Speech perception was said to rest on categorical perception abilities special to the language system. These abilities are present very early. Infants were said to hear all contrasts because they have innate, language-general knowledge about speech.","end":"So in this narrative, learning only selects among innately specified contrasts and narrows the inventory to the ones meaningful in the native language."}],"questions":[{"q":"Eimas and colleagues (1971) compared 20D and 20S changes in VOT. What did they find, and why does the comparison matter?","a":"Four-month-olds increased their sucking after a 20 ms change that crossed an adult category boundary, but not after a 20 ms change inside one category. The two changes were the same physical size. So the recovery came from crossing the boundary, which means infants perceive VOT categorically.","refs":["speech1-25","development1-05","review-33"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"Why was the Eimas result a big deal?","a":"It was a big deal because categorical perception was considered the first step in turning speech into a symbolic format. Four-month-olds are really little, with color vision still developing, and they are only starting to make vowel-like sounds. So an apparently language-specific ability this early seemed like evidence for innateness.","refs":["development1-06","review-34"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"What is perceptual narrowing, and how is it related to categorical perception?","a":"Perceptual narrowing is the decline in the ability to hear speech contrasts that the native language does not use. Werker and Tees (1984) found English-learning infants discriminate Hindi and Salish contrasts at 6 to 8 months but rarely by 10 to 12 months. It relates to categorical perception because the non-native difference comes to fall inside one native category, where differences are hard to hear.","refs":["speech1-26","development1-07","review-35"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"What is the citizens-of-the-world narrative about infant speech perception?","a":"The narrative says speech perception depends on categorical perception abilities that are special to the language system and present very early. Infants start as “citizens of the world,” able to hear all contrasts because of innate, language-general knowledge. Learning then only selects the contrasts that are meaningful in the native language and narrows the rest away.","refs":["development1-08","review-36"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"Why did Chomsky argue that behaviorist learning could not explain language acquisition?","a":"Chomsky argued this because language is generative, so learning it means finding the abstract grammar behind the sentences. Grammatical and ungrammatical sentences are not similar in any simple way. Children are also not reliably rewarded or punished for grammar, so the situation does not look like operant conditioning.","refs":["development1-02"],"tier":"Possible"}]},{"id":"m2-learning-categories","title":"Problems for the innate story and how categories are learned","tier":"Possible","blocks":[{"refs":["speech1-28","development1-09"],"main":"**The citizens-of-the-world narrative runs into four problems.** Categorical perception is not limited to language. It is not limited to humans. It is **task-dependent**, so results look more or less categorical depending on the task. And infant speech perception is not actually universal.","end":"So a full account has to explain contrasts that improve with experience as well as contrasts that are lost."},{"refs":["speech1-29","development1-10"],"main":"**Cutting and Rosner (1974) found categorical perception for sounds that are not speech.** The sounds varied in **rise time** (how quickly a sound reaches full loudness). A short rise time sounds plucked, and a long rise time sounds bowed. Listeners switched from pluck to bow over a narrow range, and discrimination peaked right at that switch.","end":"So categorical perception can arise for sounds that have nothing to do with language."},{"refs":["speech1-30","development1-11"],"main":"**Jusczyk and colleagues (1977) found that infants also perceive non-speech sounds categorically.** Using high amplitude sucking, sucking recovered after a change between categories. It stayed low after a change within a category or after no change.","end":"So infants’ categorical responses may come from general auditory processing, because plucked and bowed sounds involve no knowledge of phonemes."},{"refs":["speech1-31","development1-12"],"main":"**Chinchillas sort a speech continuum into categories much like humans do.** In **Ohlemiller and colleagues (1999)**, following **Kuhl and Miller (1975)**, chinchillas were trained with operant conditioning. Either the 0 ms lag sound or the 80 ms lag sound was the **SD** (a discriminative stimulus, a signal that food is coming).","sub":["Tested along the /ga/ to /ka/ continuum, the chinchillas switched labels over a narrow range of VOTs. Their curves had the same steep shape as the human curves."],"end":"So categorical perception of speech is not special to human language."},{"refs":["speech1-32","development1-13","development1-14"],"main":"**Narayan and colleagues (2010) found that some contrasts start out hard for infants and need exposure to become salient.** A **salient** contrast is one that is easy to notice. English-hearing four-month-olds noticed /ma/ versus /na/. They did not notice /na/ versus /ŋa/, a place contrast for nasals at the start of a syllable.","sub":["Filipino-learning infants did not notice /na/ versus /ŋa/ at 6 to 8 months, even though Filipino uses it. By 10 to 12 months they did."],"end":"So not all speech contrasts are available innately, and exposure to a contrast is necessary for acquisition in some cases."},{"refs":["development1-15","development1-16"],"main":"**Emergentist approaches say associative learning picks up statistical regularities in the environment.** **Associative learning** means learning that two things go together because they keep occurring together. In conditioning, a bell paired with food strengthens the synapse from the bell neuron to the salivation neuron.","sub":["The same kind of strengthening could support **pattern recognition**, such as learning vowel categories.","When **Kuhl and others** talk about learning the statistics of distributions, they mean things like the American vowel plot. A prototypical /a/ would be the mean, or perhaps the mode, of all the /a/ sounds a listener hears."],"end":"So categories could grow out of experience instead of being specified in advance."},{"refs":["development1-17"],"main":"**Maye and colleagues (2002) found that a few minutes of listening to a distribution changes how infants group sounds.** Infants listened passively to sounds from a [da] to [ta] VOT continuum. A **bimodal** group mostly heard sounds near each end, which is evidence for two categories. A **unimodal** group mostly heard middle sounds, which is evidence for one category.","sub":["At test, infants heard stimuli 3 and 6 in alternating or non-alternating trials.","Only the bimodal infants looked differently on the two kinds of trials."],"end":"So the unimodal infants acted as if they had learned to treat stimuli 3 and 6 as one category."},{"refs":["development1-18","development1-19"],"main":"**Social interaction matters for learning speech sounds.** The same exposure to a Mandarin speaker taught nine-month-olds a Mandarin contrast when it happened live. It did not work when the same speaker was seen on video or heard as audio. Infants whose mothers responded right after their sounds also produced more syllable-like sounds.","sub":["One idea is that humans find social interaction innately **reinforcing** (rewarding). Another is that humans are innately good at using social cues to learn from other people."],"end":"So statistical learning works within **constraints**, including which properties can be contrastive, what was learned earlier, and the social context of learning."}],"questions":[{"q":"What problems face the view that infants start with innate categories for every speech contrast?","a":"Four problems face it. Categorical perception is not limited to language, it is not limited to humans, and it is task-dependent. Infant speech perception is also not universal, because some contrasts start out hard to hear and need exposure.","refs":["speech1-28","development1-09"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"What did Cutting and Rosner (1974) find, and why does it matter for the claim that categorical perception is special to language?","a":"They found categorical perception for plucked and bowed sounds that differed in rise time. Labels switched over a narrow range, and discrimination peaked at that switch. Since these sounds are not speech, categorical perception cannot be a property of the language system alone.","refs":["speech1-29","development1-10"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"Why do the chinchilla results matter?","a":"They matter because chinchillas have no human language, yet they sorted a /ga/ to /ka/ continuum with the same steep curve as humans. Ohlemiller and colleagues (1999), following Kuhl and Miller (1975), trained them with operant conditioning on the endpoints. So categorical perception of speech is not limited to humans.","refs":["speech1-31","development1-12"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"What did Narayan and colleagues (2010) find for /na/ versus /ŋa/?","a":"They found that four-month-olds do not readily perceive /na/ versus /ŋa/, whatever language they hear. Filipino-learning infants, whose language uses the contrast, still missed it at 6 to 8 months but detected it by 10 to 12 months. So exposure is necessary to build some contrasts, and not all contrasts are available innately.","refs":["development1-13","development1-14","speech1-32"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"What did Maye and colleagues (2002) do, and what does it suggest?","a":"They had infants listen passively to either a bimodal or a unimodal distribution of VOT sounds. Only the bimodal infants later treated stimuli 3 and 6 as different. This suggests infants learn speech categories by tracking the statistics of the sounds they hear.","refs":["development1-17"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"What does it mean that statistical learning is constrained?","a":"It means learning from the input is guided and limited in several ways. Only some properties can be contrastive, so speaker voice and speech rate are not treated as categories. Earlier learning shapes what is easy to learn next, and social, interactive settings make learning work better.","refs":["development1-19","development1-18"],"tier":"Possible"}]},{"id":"m2-cascades","title":"Klatt’s model, thresholds and cascades","tier":"On the review deck","blocks":[{"refs":["speech2-02"],"main":"**Klatt (1979) describes a very old and very general model in which speech is recognized through a chain of stages.** The **speech waveform** (the sound signal itself) enters the **peripheral auditory system**, which does a **spectral analysis** (splitting sound into its frequencies). **Acoustic property detectors** then respond to that analysis, and their output is the detector outputs over time.","sub":["**Phonetic feature detectors** turn those outputs into **feature probabilities** over time, such as how likely the sound is voiced.","**Segmental analysis** combines the features into a **segmental feature matrix**, a sequence of phonemes.","**Lexical search** matches that sequence to stored words, and its output is a set of **lexical hypotheses** (candidate words).","Two problems challenge this simple view, **cascaded processing** and **feedback**."],"end":"Information is lost between the analog signal and the activation of phonemes. This loss is helpful because it lets the system contact higher levels, so words are identified from their phonemes."},{"refs":["speech2-03","speech2-04","speech2-06","speech2-07"],"main":"**In a threshold model, activity passes to the next level only when a threshold is crossed, so categorical perception arises at phoneme identification.** Feature evidence for voicing can swing between +voiced and minus voiced. The phoneme level stays silent until the evidence crosses a threshold. Then /b/ or /p/ switches on all at once.","sub":["The beach to peach continuum is a familiar voicing continuum with a sharp boundary.","The **lumber mill** metaphor fits this model. A mill turns trees into lumber, a factory turns lumber into unassembled furniture, and an IKEA customer assembles it. Each layer can only take input that the layer before has completely transformed."],"end":"So in a threshold model, two different sounds that both become /b/ are identical by the time they reach word recognition."},{"refs":["speech2-05","speech2-08","speech2-09"],"main":"**In a cascaded model, activity passes forward continuously, so partial evidence reaches the next level before any decision.** As +voiced evidence grows, /b/ activation grows with it. A sound near the boundary partly activates both /b/ and /p/. Categorical perception might happen later, or might even be an **artifact** of the task (a result created by how it is measured).","sub":["The **food web** metaphor fits this model. A drought that cuts the plants immediately affects the kangaroo rats, which affects the snakes.","The food web also has feedback loops, since adding hawks changes the numbers of snakes, lizards and rats."],"end":"So the question is whether phoneme identification for word recognition works like the lumber mill or like the food web."},{"refs":["speech2-10","speech2-11","speech2-12","speech2-13"],"main":"**McMurray, Tanenhaus, Aslin and Spivey (2003) found that eye movements reveal gradient ambiguity even when clicks look categorical.** Listeners heard a word from a /b/ to /p/ continuum in a **visual world** display. They clicked the matching image, such as a bear or a pear. Eye tracking recorded where they looked while deciding.","sub":["The clicks gave a typical step-like identification function.","On trials that all ended with a bear click, sounds closer to the boundary drew more looks to the pear. The same held in reverse for pear clicks."],"end":"So ambiguity is gradient, which means the system keeps track of how close a sound was to the boundary."},{"refs":["speech2-14","speech2-16","review-6"],"main":"**This result fits cascaded activation from features to phonemes, and it is hard to explain with thresholds.** Even stimuli labeled /b/ every time can vary in how strongly the voiced feature is activated. If a sound is clearly +bilabial and +stop, weak voicing creates momentary ambiguity between /b/ and /p/. That ambiguity appears in the eye movements.","sub":["In a threshold model, every /b/ sends the same finished phoneme to the word level. So looks to the pear should not rise gradually near the boundary.","The **TRACE** model passes activity in a cascade from a feature layer to a phoneme layer to a word layer."],"end":"So looks to the other member of a **minimal pair** (two words that differ in one sound) count as evidence for cascaded processing."},{"refs":["speech2-15"],"main":"**Andruski and colleagues (1994) found that partial input can activate word meanings.** They used **semantic priming**, in which a word is recognized faster after a word related in meaning. The nonword “beace” primed “war,” and “ging” primed “queen.”","sub":["Priming was measured with a **lexical decision task**, deciding quickly whether a target is a real word.","In word to word items such as “bill” priming “drug,” the altered prime also activates a competing real word."],"end":"So lexical representations can be activated even by incomplete or imperfect input."}],"questions":[{"q":"Describe the stages of Klatt’s (1979) model of speech perception.","a":"The speech waveform first goes through the peripheral auditory system, which splits it into frequencies. Acoustic property detectors and then phonetic feature detectors pick out properties and features such as voicing. Segmental analysis turns the features into phonemes, and lexical search matches the phonemes to stored words.","refs":["speech2-02"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"How do threshold and cascaded models differ in where categorical perception arises?","a":"In a threshold model, categorical perception arises at phoneme identification, because nothing passes forward until a threshold is crossed. In a cascaded model, activity passes forward continuously, so phonemes and words receive graded evidence. Categorical perception might then happen later, or be an artifact of a task that forces one answer.","refs":["speech2-04","speech2-05"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"What did McMurray and colleagues (2003) find with bear and pear, and why is it evidence for cascaded processing?","a":"They found that clicks were step-like, but looks to the competitor rose gradually as VOT approached the boundary. A threshold model would send the same finished /b/ to the word level for every /b/, so competitor looks would not change. The gradual increase means graded feature activation reaches the word level, which is cascaded processing.","refs":["speech2-12","speech2-16","review-6"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"Explain the lumber mill and food web metaphors.","a":"The lumber mill stands for thresholded processing, because each layer can only start once it receives a completely finished product from the layer before. The food web stands for cascaded processing, because a change in one level, such as fewer plants, immediately affects the levels that depend on it. The food web also has feedback loops, like hawks affecting snakes, lizards and rats.","refs":["speech2-06","speech2-07","speech2-08"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"What did Andruski and colleagues (1994) find?","a":"They found semantic priming from nonwords, so “beace” primed “war” and “ging” primed “queen.” The nonwords were close enough to peace and king to partly activate those words and their meanings. So lexical representations can be activated even by incomplete input, which fits cascaded processing.","refs":["speech2-15"],"tier":"Possible"}]},{"id":"m2-feedback-trace","title":"Lexical feedback, the Ganong effect and TRACE","tier":"On the review deck","blocks":[{"refs":["speech2-17","speech2-18","review-7"],"main":"**TRACE also has feedback from the word layer to the phoneme layer, and the Ganong effect is the reason for it.** **Feedback** is influence traveling from a higher level back down to a lower one. **Ganong (1980)** asked how phoneme identification is affected by word knowledge. He built word and nonword pairs such as “corpse” and “gorpse,” with continua crossing the boundary in 5 ms steps.","end":"So the test is whether knowing which end makes a real word changes how an ambiguous first sound is heard."},{"refs":["speech2-19","speech2-20","review-8","review-9"],"main":"**Two accounts make different predictions about where the lexical effect should appear.** In a late **correction** account, categorical perception works as usual, but there is a bias to report having heard a word. That bias would push responses toward the word by the same amount at every step.","sub":["In a **boundary shift** account, word knowledge moves the category boundary itself.","The lexical effect would then be strongest near the boundary, because only there can lexical information resolve ambiguity about the first phoneme. At the endpoints, the acoustics already settle the answer."],"end":"So a flat effect points to a reporting bias, and a peaked effect points to a shifted boundary."},{"refs":["speech2-21","review-10"],"main":"**Ganong’s results were strikingly consistent with the boundary shift account.** Word and nonword conditions gave nearly the same answers at the ends of the continuum. The lexical effect was largest near the boundary. Ganong considered guessing strategies, but later models stressed feedback.","end":"So word knowledge changes categorization most where the acoustic evidence is weakest."},{"refs":["speech2-22","speech2-23","review-11","review-12"],"main":"**A TRACE simulation explains the Ganong effect through top-down feedback from words to phonemes.** The input starts with a sound ambiguous between /b/ and /p/, followed by /l/, /ʌ/ and /g/. In the first frame, /b/ and /p/ are both weakly active. By the third frame, plug, blood and blush are all active because they fit the beginning.","sub":["The final /g/ is highly inconsistent with blood and blush, so activity accumulates for plug.","Plug then sends feedback to the phoneme level, which gives more activation to /p/, the phoneme consistent with the word."],"end":"So TRACE explains word effects on phoneme category boundaries through **top-down processing** (higher-level knowledge influencing lower-level analysis)."},{"refs":["speech2-24","speech2-25"],"main":"**TRACE is a version of the Interactive Activation model of written word recognition, adapted for speech that unfolds over time.** In the written model, features connect to letters and letters connect to words. Words send support back down to their letters. Spoken sounds arrive one at a time, so TRACE repeats its units across time.","sub":["Categorical perception still holds, since equal physical differences are harder to hear within a category.","But phoneme ambiguity affects word recognition, and word information affects phoneme categorization."],"end":"So the word recognition system does not sit waiting for the phoneme system to finish, like a lumber mill. Spoken word recognition is more interactive than that."},{"refs":["speech2-27","speech2-28"],"main":"**Allopenna and colleagues (1994) found that spoken word recognition is very fast and that words sharing a beginning compete early.** Listeners heard a target such as “beaker” while viewing a beaker, a beetle, a speaker and a carriage. Looks to the beaker and to the **cohort** competitor beetle rose together at first. Looks to the beetle then fell once the sounds stopped matching.","sub":["Looks to the **rhyme** competitor speaker rose later and stayed lower. Looks to the unrelated carriage stayed near zero.","A TRACE simulation closely matched these eye movements over time."],"end":"So eye movements reveal processing before a final response, much like the graded effects of category goodness in categorical perception."}],"questions":[{"q":"What did Ganong (1980) do, and what did he find?","a":"Ganong built continua between a word and a nonword, such as corpse and gorpse, in 5 ms VOT steps. Listeners heard ambiguous first sounds as the sound that made a real word. The lexical effect was largest near the category boundary and small at the endpoints, so word knowledge shifted the boundary itself.","refs":["speech2-18","speech2-21","review-10"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"How do the late correction and boundary shift accounts differ in their predictions for the Ganong effect?","a":"The late correction account predicts a flat lexical effect, the same size at every step, because it is a bias added after perception. The boundary shift account predicts an effect that peaks near the boundary. This is because only ambiguous sounds leave room for word knowledge to decide the first phoneme.","refs":["speech2-19","speech2-20","review-8","review-9"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"How does TRACE explain why an ambiguous /b/ or /p/ followed by /lʌg/ is heard as /p/?","a":"TRACE explains it through feedback from the word level. Early on, plug, blood and blush are all active, but the final /g/ fits only plug, so plug gains activation. Plug then feeds activation back to /p/, the phoneme consistent with the word, so the first sound is heard as /p/.","refs":["speech2-22","speech2-23","review-12"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"How is feedback different from cascading?","a":"Cascading sends incomplete evidence forward, from features to phonemes and from phonemes to words. Feedback sends influence backward, from words down to phonemes. TRACE has both, and the Ganong effect is the reason for including feedback.","refs":["speech2-17","speech2-25","review-7"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"What is the cohort effect found by Allopenna and colleagues (1994)?","a":"The cohort effect is early competition from words that share their beginning with the spoken word. Hearing “beaker,” people looked at the beetle about as much as the beaker at first, and then looks to the beetle fell. The rhyme speaker drew later and smaller looks, so recognition starts while the word is still unfolding.","refs":["speech2-27","speech2-28"],"tier":"On the review deck"}]},{"id":"m2-garden-path","title":"Garden paths, an autonomous parser, and early interaction","tier":"On the review deck","blocks":[{"refs":["sentence-02","sentence-03"],"main":"**“The horse raced past the barn fell” is grammatical but very hard to process, which is the garden path effect.** Readers take raced as the main verb. When fell arrives, that structure has no place for it. The sentence really describes two events, someone racing the horse and the horse falling.","sub":["It can be disambiguated as “The horse that was raced past the barn fell.”","A **garden path sentence** leads the reader into one reading that later words reveal to be wrong."],"end":"So grammaticality and processing difficulty are different things."},{"refs":["sentence-04","sentence-05","review-13","review-14"],"main":"**A parser that builds syntactic trees must work with limited memory and time, so it may prefer smaller trees.** A **parser** is the mental process that builds a syntactic tree for each sentence. The grammatical tree for the horse sentence has many more **nodes** (points in the tree for groupings such as noun phrases) than the failed parse.","sub":["Preferring smaller trees at first, to save space, is called **minimal attachment**.","Minimal attachment favors raced as the main verb, so the reader must rebuild the tree when fell arrives."],"end":"So the shortcut lightens the load most of the time but sends the reader down the garden path here."},{"refs":["sentence-06","sentence-07","sentence-08","review-15","review-16","review-17"],"main":"**Late closure is a bias to keep the current clause open as long as possible.** In “The burglar blew open the safe with the dynamite,” the phrase “with the dynamite” attaches to the big verb phrase starting with “blew open.” That makes the dynamite the tool.","sub":["In “The burglar blew open the safe with the jewels,” the sensible reading needs the unpreferred attachment to “the safe.”","Reading times and eye movements are slower for such sentences. Readers briefly treat the jewels as the tool and then retrace their steps."],"end":"So this account rests on a parser that deals only with abstract syntax, like the lumber mill, and ignores ambiguity until it becomes a problem."},{"refs":["sentence-09","sentence-10","sentence-11"],"main":"**Syntactically “difficult” sentences are not always hard, which is a problem for a purely syntactic account.** Research papers are full of **reduced relative** clauses, relative clauses with “that was” left out, as in “Recent research reported by Ferreira and Clifton.” Many come from papers that used reduced relatives as evidence for an **autonomous parser**.","sub":["**MacDonald, Perlmutter and Seidenberg (1994)** argued that semantic factors and statistical regularities interact with parsing from very early on.","Research is inanimate, so it is unlikely to do the reporting. Reported also occurs often in reduced relatives, especially after research in scientific papers."],"end":"So “The burglar arrested in the parking lot was charged today” is easy to read. “The policeman arrested in the parking lot was charged today” leads down a garden path, because policemen usually do the arresting."},{"refs":["sentence-12","sentence-13","sentence-14","sentence-15","review-18","review-19","review-20"],"main":"**Spivey and colleagues (1995) found that the visual scene changes the first parse of a spoken sentence.** People saw a pencil, a box, an empty towel and an apple on a towel. They heard “Put the apple on the towel in the box.” With minimal attachment, “on the towel” first seems like the destination, and people did look at the empty towel.","sub":["With two apples in the display, it is felicitous (natural) to say which apple is meant. So people took “on the towel” as describing the apple and looked at the empty towel much less.","Different parses of the same sentence were favored under different semantic and pragmatic conditions, and the effect happened very early."],"end":"So the result is a big problem for purely syntactic accounts, because context shaped the parse while the sentence was still unfolding."},{"refs":["sentence-16"],"main":"**Farmer and colleagues (2007) found that mouse movements reveal the same competition in the action itself.** Participants carried out the apple and towel instructions with a computer mouse while its path was tracked. With one apple, the hand curved toward the empty towel on ambiguous instructions before reaching the box. With two apples, the paths for ambiguous and unambiguous instructions were nearly the same.","end":"So syntactic ambiguity and visual context influence the actual execution of arm movements, not only eye movements."}],"questions":[{"q":"Why is “The horse raced past the barn fell” so hard, and what events does it describe?","a":"It is hard because readers first take raced as the main verb, and then fell has nowhere to attach. The sentence describes two events, someone racing the horse past the barn and the horse falling. Adding “that was” before raced disambiguates it.","refs":["sentence-02","sentence-03"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"What is minimal attachment, and why would a parser use it?","a":"Minimal attachment is a strategy of initially preferring the smaller syntactic tree, the one with fewer nodes. A parser would use it because it works with limited memory and time constraints. In the horse sentence, the smaller tree makes raced the main verb, which leads down the garden path.","refs":["sentence-04","sentence-05","review-13","review-14"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"How does late closure explain the difference between the dynamite and jewels sentences?","a":"Late closure keeps the current clause open, so “with the …” attaches to the verb phrase “blew open.” For dynamite this preferred parse is sensible, so the sentence is easy. For jewels the sensible reading needs the unpreferred attachment to “the safe,” so readers slow down and retrace their steps.","refs":["sentence-06","sentence-07","review-15","review-16"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"What did MacDonald, Perlmutter and Seidenberg (1994) argue, and how do the burglar and policeman sentences support it?","a":"They argued that meaning and statistical regularities interact with parsing from very early on. “The burglar arrested in the parking lot was charged today” is easy because burglars are usually the ones arrested. The policeman version is a garden path because policemen usually do the arresting, even though both sentences have the same syntax.","refs":["sentence-09","sentence-11"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"In Spivey and colleagues (1995), how did one apple versus two apples change where people looked?","a":"With one apple, people looked at the empty towel, because “on the towel” first seemed to be the destination. With two apples, it is felicitous to say which apple is meant, so “on the towel” was taken as describing the apple. People then looked at the empty towel much less.","refs":["sentence-12","sentence-14","review-18","review-20"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"Why are the Spivey results a big problem for purely syntactic accounts?","a":"They are a problem because the same sentence was parsed differently under different visual contexts. The difference appeared in eye movements while the sentence was being heard. A parser that always builds the same first structure from syntax alone cannot predict that.","refs":["sentence-15","sentence-13"],"tier":"On the review deck"}]},{"id":"m2-elan","title":"Word category, the ELAN and form typicality","tier":"Possible","blocks":[{"refs":["sentence-18","sentence-19","sentence-20","sentence-21","sentence-22","sentence-23"],"main":"**A parser predicts the grammatical category of the next word as a sentence unfolds.** After “The,” it expects a noun phrase. “Pizza” completes it, and “was” opens several new options. “In” starts a prepositional phrase, and the next “the” sets up a strong expectation for a noun.","sub":["“Eaten” is a past participle, a verb form, so “The pizza was in the eaten” is a **word category violation**.","It differs from an odd meaning such as “The pizza was in the ocean,” which is grammatical because ocean is a noun."],"end":"So comparing category errors with meaning errors lets researchers ask whether grammar and meaning are handled differently in the brain."},{"refs":["sentence-24"],"main":"**The early left anterior negativity (ELAN) is an ERP response to word class violations.** An **ERP** (event-related potential) is electrical brain activity recorded at the scalp and timed to an event. Classic ELAN sentences are “The pizza was in the eaten” and “The scientist criticized Max’s of proof the theorem.”","sub":["The ELAN is early, at about 100 to 200 ms.","It is left and anterior, which raised the question of whether it is specific to language.","It appears specifically for word category violations, such as a noun where a verb is expected."],"end":"So it was taken as evidence for a very fast syntactic check on word categories."},{"refs":["sentence-25"],"main":"**With visually presented words, the ELAN came from visual cortex.** **Dikker, Rabagliati, Farmer and Pylkkänen (2010)** tied it to **form typicality**. When a verb is expected, the prediction may feed back to the earliest parts of the visual system and predict how verbs are written.","end":"So the very early response may reflect a mismatch between that prediction and what finally appears on the screen."},{"refs":["sentence-26","sentence-27","sentence-28"],"main":"**Words like perches and needs can be nouns or verbs, and their form makes one reading easier.** **Perches** is noun-like and **needs** is verb-like in **phonological form typicality** (how much a word sounds like typical nouns or verbs). Reading slowed at disambiguation when the needed category clashed with the form.","sub":["“The bird perches seem easy to install” uses perches as a noun, which matches its form. “The bird perches comfortably in the cage” uses it as a verb, against its form.","“The student needs to be more focused” fits the verb-like form of needs. “The student needs were not being met” goes against it."],"end":"So parsing uses learned statistics about how word forms sound, along with the structure the sentence requires."},{"refs":["sentence-29","sentence-30"],"main":"**The ELAN for a noun where a verb is expected appears only when the noun carries physical cues to its category.** The cue can be syntactic, such as a noun suffix, or statistical, such as a noun-like form. No effect appeared for neutral nouns like “infant” that lack strong cues.","sub":["Four sentences sum up sentence processing. “Colorless green ideas sleep furiously” says syntax is somewhat independent of semantics. “The horse raced past the barn fell” says parsing takes shortcuts to save memory.","“Put the apple on the towel in the box” says context interacts with syntax very early. “The bird perches were comfortable” says the earliest brain effects are driven partly by form typicality."],"end":"So the earliest syntactic effects seem tied to predictions about the physical forms of words."}],"questions":[{"q":"What is the ELAN, and what are its main features?","a":"The ELAN, the early left anterior negativity, is an ERP response to word class violations. It is early, at about 100 to 200 ms, and it is strongest over the left front of the head. It appears specifically when a word has the wrong category, such as “The pizza was in the eaten.”","refs":["sentence-24"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"Why is “The pizza was in the eaten” a different kind of error from “The pizza was in the ocean”?","a":"“The pizza was in the eaten” is a word category violation, because eaten is a verb form where a noun is required. “The pizza was in the ocean” is only odd in meaning, since ocean is a noun and the grammar is fine. So the two can be compared to see whether grammar and meaning are handled differently.","refs":["sentence-23","sentence-22"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"What is form typicality, and how did perches and needs reveal its effect?","a":"Form typicality is how much a word sounds like typical nouns or typical verbs. Perches is noun-like and needs is verb-like. Reading slowed when the sentence required the category that did not match the form, such as perches used as a verb.","refs":["sentence-27","sentence-28"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"Why did neutral nouns like “infant” produce no early effect when a verb was expected?","a":"Infant has no noun suffix and no noun-like form, so it carries no physical cue to its category. The early effect appeared only when syntactic or form-based cues were physically available in the word. So the earliest response seems to reflect a mismatch with predicted word forms, not an abstract category check.","refs":["sentence-29","sentence-25"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"Summarize sentence processing with the four example sentences.","a":"“Colorless green ideas sleep furiously” means syntax is somewhat independent of semantics. “The horse raced past the barn fell” means parsing takes shortcuts to save memory. “Put the apple on the towel in the box” means context interacts with syntax very early. “The bird perches were comfortable” means the earliest brain effects are driven partly by form typicality.","refs":["sentence-30"],"tier":"Possible"}]},{"id":"m2-aphasia-models","title":"Aphasia models and the Broca and Wernicke dichotomy","tier":"On the review deck","blocks":[{"refs":["aphasias-02"],"main":"**In the 1870s, Lichtheim and Wernicke explained aphasias as disconnections between centers.** **Aphasia** is a loss of language caused by brain damage. In Lichtheim’s model, **A** holds the sound images of words, **M** holds the movement images for saying them, and **B** holds concepts. A disconnection between A and B could cause a loss of understanding spoken words.","sub":["A disconnection between A and M could cause a loss of verbatim repetition.","This box and arrow approach is foundational for modeling typical language too, as in the interactive activation model."],"end":"So each specific loss of function was tied to a specific broken pathway."},{"refs":["aphasias-03"],"main":"**Norman Geschwind revived this approach in the 1970s as the Geschwind-Wernicke model.** It ties language to **Broca’s area** in the left frontal lobe, **Wernicke’s area** in the temporal lobe, and the **arcuate fasciculus** connecting them. In the century between Wernicke and Geschwind, linguistics changed dramatically.","sub":["Syntax came to be seen as independent of other parts of language, with its own computational principles.","That opened the door to theorizing about **double dissociations**, where one patient loses one ability and keeps another, while another patient shows the reverse."],"end":"So aphasias could now be described as damage to specific parts of language."},{"refs":["aphasias-04","aphasias-05","review-21"],"main":"**Broca’s aphasia gives halting telegraphic speech, and Wernicke’s aphasia gives fluent but empty speech.** Broca’s patients have word-finding pauses, lose **grammatical morphemes** (small grammar words and endings), and often disturb word order. Their important content words are relatively spared. Their everyday comprehension seems good, but controlled testing reveals impairments.","sub":["Wernicke’s patients speak fluently with good intonation but produce **word salad** or “Jabberwocky,” often with **neologisms** (invented words). Their syntax is roughly intact, but their comprehension is poor.","**Agrammatism** is almost a synonym for Broca’s aphasia, but some patients with lesions in Broca’s area are not agrammatic."],"end":"So the two aphasias look like mirror images, which made an attractive dichotomy."},{"refs":["aphasias-06","aphasias-07","aphasias-08","review-22","review-23"],"main":"**The dichotomy says Wernicke’s aphasia is a problem with activation in the lexicon and Broca’s aphasia is a problem with grammar.** It fits the sharp distinction that late 20th century linguistics drew between the **grammar** and the **lexicon** (the store of words). That distinction starts with Chomsky’s view of language learning as a computational problem and his evidence that syntax is independent of semantics.","sub":["This is fairly distant from how Broca described his original cases. His patient Tan could produce only one syllable but seemed to understand speech.","Broca’s actual patients were arguably unable to speak at all because of motor control problems. Today they would be classified with **apraxia of speech**, not Broca’s aphasia.","The dichotomy also matches the speech production model. **Lemmas** at the functional level have an arbitrary link to their referents, while trees and affixes at the positional level come from abstract syntactic processes.","Words like HOUSEBOAT and DOGHOUSE are not arbitrary in meaning. But the speech error “easy enoughly” for “easily enough” places derivational morphology at the positional level."],"end":"So the split between lexicon and grammar seemed to line up with brain damage, with linguistic theory and with speech production."},{"refs":["aphasias-09","aphasias-10"],"main":"**To show a grammar deficit, researchers had to test comprehension with carefully built sentences.** Popular sentence processing theories compute syntax independently of meaning. So Broca’s patients might generally understand what is said and still have a core grammatical deficit.","sub":["Broca, in the 1860s, only needed to separate articulate speech from comprehension.","A syntax deficit needs a test that patients can answer without speaking."],"end":"So comprehension testing became the way to look for a deficit in grammar itself."}],"questions":[{"q":"How did Lichtheim and Wernicke explain different aphasias?","a":"They explained them as disconnections between centers. In Lichtheim’s model, A holds sound images, M holds movement images and B holds concepts. A break between A and B would impair understanding spoken words, and a break between A and M would impair verbatim repetition.","refs":["aphasias-02"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"What changed between Wernicke and Geschwind that made new theories of aphasia possible?","a":"Linguistics changed dramatically. Syntax came to be treated as independent of other parts of language, with its own computational principles. That opened the door to describing double dissociations between specific language functions.","refs":["aphasias-03"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"Contrast the speech of Broca’s aphasia and Wernicke’s aphasia.","a":"Broca’s aphasia produces telegraphic speech, with trouble in word order and grammatical particles but relative sparing of content words. Wernicke’s aphasia produces fluent word salad, often with neologisms, while syntax stays roughly intact. Wernicke’s patients also have poor comprehension.","refs":["aphasias-04","aphasias-05","review-21"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"Why is it confusing to call Broca’s aphasia a grammar problem, given Broca’s own patients?","a":"It is confusing because Broca’s actual patients would not necessarily count as Broca’s aphasics today. They were arguably unable to produce speech at all, apparently because of motor control problems. They would now be diagnosed with apraxia of speech.","refs":["aphasias-06","review-22"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"Why was the grammar versus lexicon dichotomy for aphasia attractive?","a":"It was attractive because it matched a sharp distinction in late 20th century formal linguistics between the grammar and the lexicon. That distinction began with Chomsky’s account of language learning as a computational problem. His examples, such as “Colorless green ideas sleep furiously,” supported the independence of syntax from semantics.","refs":["aphasias-07","review-23"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"How does the slip “easy enoughly” answer the objection about words like HOUSEBOAT and DOGHOUSE?","a":"It answers the objection because the suffix moved as a separate piece. The speaker meant “easily enough,” but -ly attached to the wrong word. So derivational morphology is done at the positional level by grammatical processes, even when a word’s meaning is not arbitrary.","refs":["aphasias-08"],"tier":"Possible"}]},{"id":"m2-broca-comprehension","title":"Testing Broca’s aphasia, syntax or performance","tier":"On the review deck","blocks":[{"refs":["aphasias-11","aphasias-12","review-24"],"main":"**It mattered whether Broca’s patients have comprehension deficits, because their speech problems could be purely motor.** Broca’s aphasia almost always comes with some motor dysfunction. Agrammatic speech might just be an attempt to communicate despite motor limits. **Hughlings Jackson** said we observe what a person can do despite the impairment.","sub":["Chomsky separated **competence** (underlying knowledge of language) from **performance** (using that knowledge). When motor problems disrupt performance, comprehension may give a clearer window into competence.","Everyday sentences like “The dog bit the man” have redundant cues. Listeners can build the likeliest event from DOG, BITE and MAN, or use **canonical word order** (agent, then verb, then undergoer)."],"end":"So a real test needs sentences like “The dog that the man is biting is angry,” where only grammar gives the right answer."},{"refs":["aphasias-13","aphasias-14","review-25","review-26"],"main":"**Caramazza and Zurif (1976) found that Broca’s patients fail when syntax conflicts with common sense.** They used a sentence-picture matching task with four sentence types. Simple controls and semantically constrained center-embedded sentences can be solved from meaning. Reversible and improbable center-embedded sentences can only be solved from grammar.","sub":["Healthy controls were about equally good on all types. Wernicke’s patients were poor overall, probably because of their general comprehension deficits.","Broca’s patients did well when meaning gave the answer away. They did poorly on reversible and improbable sentences, such as “The bird that the worm is eating is blue.”"],"end":"So this specific deficit was taken as evidence that Broca’s aphasia affects syntax specifically."},{"refs":["aphasias-15"],"main":"**A specific theory of the parts of language lets researchers isolate problems with the grammar versus the lexicon.** Instead of sorting aphasics by good or poor comprehension, or by fluency, tests can target particular aspects of language.","end":"So structure-function relationships can be explored with much more precision."},{"refs":["aphasias-16","aphasias-17","review-27","review-28"],"main":"**Dick and colleagues found the same pattern with four sentence types, but Broca’s patients were not good on simple sentences either.** Participants indicated which animal was doing the action. Active and subject cleft sentences put the doer first. Object cleft and passive sentences put the receiver first, as in “It’s the cat that the dog is biting.”","sub":["Elderly controls were near perfect on all four types.","Broca’s patients had particular difficulty with object clefts and passives, though their performance was overall not so great."],"end":"So the pattern resembled Caramazza and Zurif’s, with a general drop plus an extra cost for complex sentences."},{"refs":["aphasias-18","review-29"],"main":"**Healthy undergraduates under processing stress showed the same aphasia-like pattern.** Stress conditions included speeding up the speech, filtering it, and remembering lists of numbers as a secondary task. Object cleft and passive sentences were particularly vulnerable to all of them.","end":"So the comprehension deficits in Broca’s aphasia may be a performance issue after all, because complex sentences need more processing resources."},{"refs":["aphasias-19","aphasias-20","aphasias-21","aphasias-22","review-30","review-31"],"main":"**Saygin and colleagues (2004) found that comprehension deficits in aphasia are not purely linguistic.** Broca’s aphasics had difficulty matching an action to an object whether the action was given in words or by pantomime. Performance on the nonverbal version was significantly correlated with damage to Broca’s area.","sub":["Wernicke’s patients were particularly bad at matching environmental sounds, such as a cow mooing, to pictures.","Their verbal and nonverbal versions did not differ significantly, and deficits in both were associated with Wernicke’s area."],"end":"So damage to these areas affects meaning beyond language, which weakens the claim that the deficits are purely language-specific."},{"refs":["aphasias-23","aphasias-25"],"main":"**How language-specific aphasic deficits are is still a controversy, but the methods have come a long way since the 19th century.** Researchers now use relatively sophisticated tests of specific aspects of language. They also use quantitative analyses of behavior and of structure-function relationships.","sub":["**Voxel-based lesion-symptom mapping** relates damage at each **voxel** (a small 3D unit of an MRI image) to symptom scores across many patients.","Fluency deficits were linked to anterior regions and comprehension deficits to posterior regions."],"end":"So data from many patients fit a rough double dissociation, with posterior regions tied to perception and anterior regions tied to production."}],"questions":[{"q":"Why was it interesting to show that Broca’s patients have comprehension deficits?","a":"It was interesting because Broca’s aphasia almost always comes with motor problems, so agrammatic speech might only reflect effortful speaking. As Hughlings Jackson put it, we see what a person can do despite the impairment. Comprehension avoids the motor problem, so it gives a clearer window into competence.","refs":["aphasias-11"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"Why do we need psycholinguistic tests to observe grammatical difficulties in comprehension?","a":"We need them because conversation is full of redundant cues to what a speaker means. In “The dog bit the man,” a listener can combine DOG, BITE and MAN into the likeliest event or rely on canonical word order. Only sentences like “The dog that the man is biting is angry” force grammatical analysis.","refs":["aphasias-12","review-24"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"What did Caramazza and Zurif (1976) do and find?","a":"They gave a sentence-picture matching task with control, semantically constrained, reversible and improbable sentences. Controls did well on all of them, and Wernicke’s patients were poor overall. Broca’s patients failed mainly when syntax conflicted with common sense, which was taken as evidence that Broca’s aphasia affects syntax specifically.","refs":["aphasias-13","aphasias-14","review-25","review-26"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"What sentence types did Dick and colleagues use, and how did aphasia patients do?","a":"They used active, subject cleft, object cleft and passive sentences, and participants indicated which animal did the action. Broca’s patients had particular difficulty with object clefts and passives, where the receiver comes first. Their performance was overall not so great, even on actives.","refs":["aphasias-16","aphasias-17","review-27","review-28"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"What happened when healthy undergraduates did the Dick task under stress, and what does it suggest?","a":"Object cleft and passive sentences were particularly vulnerable to every stress condition, such as sped-up speech or remembering numbers. Healthy people with no brain damage then looked like Broca’s patients. So the deficit in Broca’s aphasia may be a performance issue rather than lost grammatical knowledge.","refs":["aphasias-18","review-29"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"Are the comprehension deficits in aphasia language-specific? Use Saygin and colleagues (2004).","a":"They do not seem to be purely language-specific. Broca’s aphasics had trouble matching actions to objects whether the stimuli were linguistic or not, and the nonverbal deficit correlated with damage to Broca’s area. Wernicke’s patients were equally poor at matching words and environmental sounds to pictures.","refs":["aphasias-19","aphasias-20","aphasias-21","review-30","review-31"],"tier":"On the review deck"}]},{"id":"m2-words-and-meanings","title":"Finding words in speech and learning what they mean","tier":"On the review deck","blocks":[{"refs":["development2-02","development2-03"],"main":"**Speech has no physical boundaries between words, so infants must learn to find words in a sea of sounds.** Pauses come from **closures** (moments when the vocal tract is fully blocked). Closures are as likely within words as between them. Several other cues help.","sub":["Some words appear in isolation, such as “Hello” and “Stop,” but not all words do.","**Phonotactics** (which sound sequences a language allows) helps, because sequences like the one in “wordboundary” rarely occur inside words.","**Jusczyk and colleagues (1999)** found that 7.5-month-olds familiarized with “kingdom” preferred the kingdom passage, but those familiarized with “device” did not. Most English words are stressed on the first syllable, so stress may signal word beginnings."],"end":"So each cue helps, but none works for every word or every language."},{"refs":["development2-04","development2-05"],"main":"**Transitional probabilities offer another cue, because syllables predict each other more strongly within words than across words.** A **transitional probability** is the likelihood that syllable B follows syllable A. In “pretty baby,” “pre” is very strongly followed by “ty.” But many syllables can follow “ty,” so “ba” is poorly predicted.","sub":["This is probabilistic. “What’s that in the road ahead” can be split as “a head,” and “I scream” sounds like “ice cream.”"],"end":"So a drop in transitional probability is a good guess for a word boundary."},{"refs":["development2-06","development2-07","development2-08","development2-09","development2-10"],"main":"**Saffran and colleagues (1996) found that infants can find words using transitional probabilities alone.** The speech was monotone, made of simple CV syllables, and presented with no pauses. So stress, phonotactics and silence gave no cues. Three-syllable words such as tokibu, gikoba and gopila ran together in one stream.","sub":["Half of the words were repeated more often. The test compared words with **part-words** (sequences spanning a word boundary) matched for frequency.","In the example stream, GOPILA and BU-GIKO each occur twice. But GI follows BU only 2 out of 4 times, while each step inside GOPILA happens 2 out of 2 times."],"end":"Infants looked longer at part-words, a **novelty effect**. So they had learned the statistical regularities of the stream, not just how often sequences occurred."},{"refs":["development2-11","development2-12","review-37"],"main":"**Once children can find words, they face an inductive problem about what each word means.** A child hearing “cat” could take it to mean cat, furry, pet, whiskers, walking, leg, Felix, animal or alive. This set of possible meanings is the **hypothesis space**.","end":"So children need ways to narrow the hypothesis space, which is the problem Quine described with “gavagai.”"},{"refs":["development2-13","development2-14","development2-15","development2-16","review-38","review-39","review-40"],"main":"**Joint attention and the speaker’s gaze help children narrow word meanings.** **Joint attention** is sharing an attentional focus with another person. **Gaze following** emerges around 9 months and signals an understanding of reference. **Baldwin and colleagues** found that 18-month-olds learned a new word when an adult interacting with them labeled their toy.","sub":["They did not learn it when a voice from behind a screen labeled the same toy.","**Baldwin** also taught new words to 16 to 17 and 18 to 19 month olds using **intentional cues** (signals of what the speaker means). In **follow-in labeling**, the adult looked at the toy the infant was focused on. In **discrepant labeling**, the adult looked at a toy in a bucket."],"end":"So hearing a word while seeing an object is not enough, because infants look for evidence of what the speaker intends."},{"refs":["development2-17","development2-18","review-41"],"main":"**Akhtar and colleagues found that infants use adults’ facial expressions to identify the intended referent of a new word.** These are **affective** (emotional) and **behavioral** cues to the speaker’s intention.","end":"So intention is conveyed through more than gaze direction."},{"refs":["development2-19","development2-20","development2-21","review-42","review-43"],"main":"**Children also bring default word learning strategies that narrow the hypothesis space.** The **whole object assumption** expects a new word to name a whole object, not a part or a property. **Mutual exclusivity** (Markman, 1989) assumes, as a default, that an object can have only one name.","sub":["The implication is that new words map to nameless objects.","Mutual exclusivity emerges during the second half of the second year."],"end":"So every word a child already knows helps narrow the meaning of the next one."},{"refs":["development2-22","development2-23","development2-24"],"main":"**Children understand more grammar than they produce.** **Menn** separates early utterances into a bunch of relevant words, formulas, word combinations and stable constructions. “Up!”, “Pick you up!” and “Mommy up!” could come from any of these, so full context is necessary but often not sufficient to tell.","sub":["Even children at the one-word stage can use basic transitive word order in comprehension.","**Gertner and colleagues (2006)** found that two-year-olds looked longer at the duck acting on the bunny after hearing “The duck is gorping the bunny.” Gorp is a made-up verb, so only word order could guide them."],"end":"So two-year-olds have learned the transitive structure itself, even though their own utterances average about two morphemes, like “Duck gorp bunny.”"}],"questions":[{"q":"What cues help infants find word boundaries, and what did Jusczyk and colleagues (1999) find?","a":"Words heard in isolation, phonotactics, stress and transitional probabilities all help. Jusczyk and colleagues found that 7.5-month-olds who heard “kingdom” preferred a passage containing it, but those who heard “device” did not. Most English words are stressed on the first syllable, so stress may mark word beginnings.","refs":["development2-03"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"How did Saffran and colleagues (1996) isolate transitional probabilities from other cues?","a":"They made the speech monotone, used only simple CV syllables, and presented the syllables with no pauses. That removed stress, phonotactic and pause cues. So the only clue to where each made-up word began and ended was how predictable the syllables were.","refs":["development2-06","development2-07"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"Why did Saffran’s test compare words with part-words matched for frequency?","a":"It did so because infants could prefer heard sequences just because they were familiar. Matching frequency makes words and part-words equally familiar, so only their transitional probabilities differ. Infants looked longer at part-words, so they had learned the statistics and not just frequency.","refs":["development2-09","development2-10"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"What is the inductive problem of early word learning?","a":"The inductive problem is that one naming event fits many possible meanings. A child hearing “cat” could take it to mean cat, furry, pet, whiskers, walking, leg, Felix, animal or alive. This is Quine’s “gavagai” problem, so children need ways to narrow the hypothesis space.","refs":["development2-12","review-37"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"What did Baldwin and colleagues find about joint attention?","a":"They found that joint attention optimizes word learning. Eighteen-month-olds learned a new word when an adult interacting with them labeled the toy they were playing with. They did not learn it when a voice from behind a screen gave the same label.","refs":["development2-14","review-39"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"What is mutual exclusivity, and how does it help a child?","a":"Mutual exclusivity (Markman, 1989) is the default assumption that an object can have only one name. So a new word is taken to name an object that has no name yet. It emerges during the second half of the second year and narrows the hypothesis space for new words.","refs":["development2-20","review-43"],"tier":"On the review deck"}]},{"id":"m2-l1-critical","title":"Critical and sensitive periods for a first language","tier":"On the review deck","blocks":[{"refs":["l1-critical-02"],"main":"**A critical period allows no learning outside a fixed window, while a sensitive period only makes learning best during a window.** A **critical period** has a discrete, programmed onset and offset. Change in response to the environment is strictly limited to that period. A **sensitive** or **optimal period** allows learning across the life span, but learning works best during particular windows.","sub":["Sensitive period windows can open and close gradually, and their timing can be affected by experience."],"end":"So any learning after the window counts against a strict critical period but fits a sensitive period."},{"refs":["l1-critical-03","l1-critical-04","l1-critical-05"],"main":"**Amblyopia is poor vision in one eye despite a healthy eye, and it comes from competition between the eyes in cortex.** Strabismus, a cataract or anisometropia can deprive one eye of normal input early in life. Then the other eye **colonizes** the cortical representation of the deprived eye. Fixing the eye later does not restore normal vision, because that eye stays at a competitive disadvantage.","sub":["Patching the good eye restores the competitive balance and lets the brain’s own **plasticity** (ability to rewire) restore function.","Patching works best early in development and may be ineffective by about age 10."],"end":"So when the problem happens matters as much as what the problem is."},{"refs":["l1-critical-06","l1-critical-07","l1-critical-08"],"main":"**Adults with amblyopia can still improve with the right training, so amblyopia is better called a sensitive period.** **Levi and Polat (1996)** found that long, intensive training on psychophysical tasks improved task performance and general vision in adults, some in their 50s. **Li, Ngo, Nguyen, Lam and Levi (2008)** found that playing video games improved visual acuity and attention in adult amblyopia.","end":"So early disturbance can cause lifelong loss and treatment works best early, yet the right experience can still reverse deficits late into adulthood."},{"refs":["l1-critical-09","l1-critical-10","l1-critical-11","l1-critical-12","review-44"],"main":"**Lenneberg (1967) proposed that brain maturation opens and closes the window for language.** In Biological Foundations of Language, he tied stage-like language milestones to motor milestones, which suggested a biological program. Brain data were sparse because they came from post-mortem tissue, with no neuron size data between ages 2 and 18.","sub":["Abrupt changes in neuron size and packing density before age two might be permissive for starting language. So the critical period seemed to open after that.","Aphasia is surprisingly rare in children with brain lesions. Children whose left hemisphere is removed in a **hemispherectomy** (surgery typically done to relieve epileptic seizures) seem to develop typical language abilities. Adults do not recover language after this surgery."],"end":"So Lenneberg hypothesized that adult-like **cerebral dominance** (left hemisphere specialization for language) closes the critical period."},{"refs":["l1-critical-13"],"main":"**Children with perinatal strokes do much better than adults, but they do not fully catch up.** A **perinatal** stroke happens between before birth and 28 days after birth. Early language deficits appear after damage to either hemisphere, and some are worse after right hemisphere damage.","end":"So outcomes in the low-normal range point to a persistent delay rather than complete recovery."},{"refs":["l1-critical-14","l1-critical-15","l1-critical-16","l1-critical-17"],"main":"**Genie learned many words after puberty but very little grammar.** **Curtiss and colleagues (1974)** studied Genie, who was isolated and punished for making sounds from 20 months of age until she was found at 13. Over four years she came to understand hundreds of words, some morphology, especially plurals, and some prepositions.","sub":["Her production lagged severely behind her comprehension, partly because she had been punished for making sounds.","Her grammar developed much more slowly than her vocabulary. She never understood “either or.”"],"end":"So the gap between her vocabulary and her grammar is widely read as evidence that first language grammar learning is impaired after a sensitive period. Questions remain about learning disability and abuse, and funding ended after four years."},{"refs":["l1-critical-18","l1-critical-19"],"main":"**Mayberry, Lock and Kazmi (2002) found that a first language learned late is learned poorly, even with a typical upbringing.** Deaf children aged 9 to 15 had difficulty learning **ASL** (American Sign Language) when it was their first language. Children who went deaf after learning a spoken language did much better. These differences persisted into adulthood.","end":"So deprivation of input does not extend the critical period. All of this evidence comes from unusual cases with no animal models, so firm conclusions are hard to reach."}],"questions":[{"q":"What is the difference between a critical period and a sensitive period?","a":"A critical period has a discrete, programmed onset and offset, and learning is impossible before or after it. A sensitive period allows learning throughout life, but learning is optimal during a window. That window can open and close gradually and can itself be shaped by experience.","refs":["l1-critical-02"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"Why does amblyopia happen, and why does patching the good eye work?","a":"Amblyopia happens because the eyes compete for cortical neurons, and an eye with poor early input loses its territory to the other eye. Patching the good eye removes its input for part of the day. So the weak eye can win back connections through the brain’s own plasticity, mostly early in development.","refs":["l1-critical-04","l1-critical-05"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"What was Lenneberg’s (1967) theory, and what evidence motivated it?","a":"Lenneberg proposed that the sensitive period depends on the development of cerebral dominance, or language laterality. One motivation was a rough alignment of brain development with language milestones. The other was that children recover language after brain injury or hemispherectomy, while adults do not.","refs":["l1-critical-10","l1-critical-12","review-44"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"What did Genie learn and not learn, and why is her case hard to interpret?","a":"Genie learned hundreds of words, some morphology such as plurals, and some prepositions. Her grammar developed much more slowly, and her speech lagged far behind her comprehension. Her case is hard to interpret because she may have had a learning disability, she suffered abuse, and funding ended after four years.","refs":["l1-critical-15","l1-critical-16","l1-critical-17"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"What did Mayberry, Lock and Kazmi (2002) find?","a":"They found that deaf children learning ASL as their first language at 9 to 15 had great difficulty. Deaf children who had learned a spoken language before going deaf learned ASL much better. The difference lasted into adulthood, so having a language early matters more than which language it is.","refs":["l1-critical-18"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"Is there a critical period or a sensitive period for L1?","a":"The evidence fits a sensitive period better, but firm conclusions are difficult. Recovery after stroke and hemispherectomy is very different in childhood and adulthood, and deprivation does not extend the period. But all the data come from highly unusual cases, and no animal models or gene knockout experiments are possible.","refs":["l1-critical-19","l1-critical-13"],"tier":"Possible"}]},{"id":"m2-transfer","title":"Transfer in second language learning","tier":"Possible","blocks":[{"refs":["l2-learning-02","l2-learning-03"],"main":"**Adults learn a second language faster at first, but children reach higher ultimate attainment.** A typical child knows about 20 words at 18 months, which would be poor progress for an adult learner. Adults start faster because of **transfer** from L1 and better general cognitive skills.","sub":["Adults rarely become native-like, and transfer may be part of the reason.","Some toddler milestones, such as spatial relations and event representations, are general concepts that adults already have. So adults can focus on how the target language expresses them."],"end":"So speed of learning and final success are separate measures."},{"refs":["l2-learning-04","l2-learning-05"],"main":"**Transfer is the use of L1 processes and representations for an L2, and it can help or hurt.** English speakers use their **alveolar** category for both Hindi dental and retroflex consonants. So Hindi minimal pairs sound the same to them.","sub":["This comes from **acquired similarity**, where sounds in one category come to sound more alike."],"end":"So in this case transfer makes the L2 contrast harder to learn."},{"refs":["l2-learning-06","l2-learning-07"],"main":"**Transfer also makes some grammar hard, such as Chinese classifiers and French gender.** A **classifier** is a word that Chinese puts between a word like one or that and the noun, as in yī gè rén. Different nouns require different classifiers, depending on the kind of thing named. Examples are objects with handles, books, wheeled vehicles, flat objects and long thin objects. In English, a chair, that dog, the car and one book need no thought about noun meaning. So English speakers have learned to ignore the cues that classifiers depend on.","sub":["**Grammatical gender** is different from social gender. Nothing is male about a book or female about liberty.","Gender controls **agreement**, so pronouns, adjectives and some verb forms must match the noun. French even has synonyms with different genders, such as auto and voiture."],"end":"So learners must attend to features their first language trained them to ignore."},{"refs":["l2-learning-08","l2-learning-09"],"main":"**Transfer is not all bad, because cognates and loan words make vocabulary easier.** **Cognates** share an etymology, and **loan words** are more recent borrowings, such as Japanese korokke and monoreru. But not all similar-sounding words are cognates.","sub":["“Arnold está embarazado” means Arnold is pregnant, not embarrassed. Such a word is a **false friend**."],"end":"So the bias to link L2 words to similar L1 words is strong, and it helps only when the meanings match."},{"refs":["l2-learning-10","l2-learning-11","l2-learning-12","l2-learning-13"],"main":"**De Groot and Keijzer (2000) found that cognates are learned faster and remembered better.** Dutch speakers learned made-up words paired with Dutch words. The made-up words varied in similarity to their Dutch partners and in **concreteness**. Examples were gitaar and gettar, a concrete cognate, and herstel and groop, an abstract non-cognate.","sub":["Retention was tested after one week with no training. Cognates and concrete words were forgotten less.","A receptive test alone would not be impressive, because gitaar, tijger and domein could be guessed. The **productive** test required recalling the made-up form itself."],"end":"So cognate status contributes to the very earliest stages of word learning in a second language."}],"questions":[{"q":"In what sense are babies overrated as language learners?","a":"Babies are overrated because adults actually learn faster at first. A child knows only about 20 words after 18 months, while adults benefit from transfer and better general cognitive skills. Adults do worse only in ultimate attainment, since they rarely become native-like.","refs":["l2-learning-02"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"What is transfer, and how does it make the Hindi dental and retroflex contrast hard for English speakers?","a":"Transfer is the use of L1 processes and representations for an L2. English speakers hear both Hindi dental and retroflex consonants through their single alveolar category. So the two sounds seem the same, and transfer makes the L2 contrast harder to learn.","refs":["l2-learning-04","l2-learning-05"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"Why are Chinese classifiers hard for native English speakers?","a":"They are hard because Chinese requires different classifiers for different kinds of nouns, such as objects with handles or flat objects. English determiners do not depend on what kind of thing a noun names. So English speakers have learned to ignore the cues that classifiers depend on.","refs":["l2-learning-06"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"What did de Groot and Keijzer (2000) do and find?","a":"Dutch speakers learned made-up words paired with Dutch words that varied in cognate status and concreteness. Cognates were learned more rapidly and remembered more easily after a week without training. So cognate status helps the very earliest stages of second language word learning.","refs":["l2-learning-10","l2-learning-11"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"Why did de Groot and Keijzer’s productive test make the study more convincing?","a":"A receptive test alone would not be impressive, because many cognate meanings, such as gitaar, could be guessed without training. The productive test asked for the made-up word itself, given the Dutch word. So a cognate advantage there reflects real learning of new forms.","refs":["l2-learning-12","l2-learning-13"],"tier":"Possible"}]},{"id":"m2-bilingual-competition","title":"Cross-language competition and the Revised Hierarchical Model","tier":"On the review deck","blocks":[{"refs":["l2-learning-14","l2-learning-15","l2-learning-16","review-49"],"main":"**Eye movements in a visual world task can reveal whether similar L1 words are active while bilinguals process L2.** In McMurray and colleagues’ study, competition between BEAR and PEAR depended on distance from the category boundary. **Allopenna and colleagues (1994)** found the **cohort effect**. Words that share a beginning with the target, such as beetle for beaker, draw early looks.","end":"So looks to an object measure how active its name is, even when the final choice is correct."},{"refs":["l2-learning-17","l2-learning-18","l2-learning-19","review-50","review-51","review-52"],"main":"**Marian and Spivey (2003) found that Russian words are active when Russian-English bilinguals listen in English, but not the reverse.** If L1 words are always activated, bilinguals hearing “speaker” in English should look at matches. The Russian word for matches, spichki, starts the same way. With one competitor present, they looked at within-language and between-language competitors to the same extent.","sub":["With both competitors present, they looked at the within-language competitor more. They still looked at the between-language competitor about twice as often as a control object.","In Russian, English words did not seem to be activated. The small difference from the control was not statistically significant."],"end":"So competition between the languages is **asymmetric**, running from L1 into L2."},{"refs":["l2-learning-20","l2-learning-21","review-53","review-54"],"main":"**Kroll and Stewart’s Revised Hierarchical Model explains the asymmetry with spreading activation.** The model has an L1 lexicon, an L2 lexicon and shared concepts. The L1 lexicon is larger and more strongly connected to concepts. The L2 lexicon has strong forward connections to the L1 lexicon, because that direction is emphasized in learning.","sub":["Here Russian is the L1 and English is the L2.","In the English task, the concept for “speaker” partly activates the Russian word for matches, spichki.","Spichki is strongly linked to the concept for matches, so that activity feeds back to the concept. Conceptual activation for matches then leads to eye movements toward the matches."],"end":"In the L1 task, the conceptual links from L2 words are too weak to create this interference reliably."},{"refs":["l2-learning-22","review-55"],"main":"**The model predicts that even balanced bilinguals are not two monolinguals in one.** For **balanced bilinguals**, conceptual links are similarly strong for both languages. So competitive dynamics and inhibition are engaged all the time.","end":"So this prediction led to much work on bilinguals’ advantages in non-linguistic **cognitive control** tasks."}],"questions":[{"q":"What was Marian and Spivey’s hypothesis, and how did they test it?","a":"They hypothesized that if L1 words are always activated, Russian speakers should look at matches when hearing “speaker” in English. The Russian word for matches, spichki, begins like speaker. They tracked eye movements to such between-language competitors in a visual world display.","refs":["l2-learning-17","review-50"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"What did Marian and Spivey find during L2 spoken word recognition?","a":"With one competitor present, bilinguals looked at within-language and between-language competitors to the same extent. With both present, they looked at the within-language competitor more. They still looked at the between-language competitor about twice as often as a control, so L1 words were active during L2 listening.","refs":["l2-learning-18","review-51"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"What did Marian and Spivey find during L1 spoken word recognition?","a":"English words did not seem to be activated when native Russian speakers did the task in Russian. The small difference between the between-language and control conditions was not statistically significant. So the interference is asymmetric.","refs":["l2-learning-19","review-52"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"What does the Revised Hierarchical Model assume about the two lexicons and their links?","a":"It assumes the L1 lexicon is larger and more strongly connected to relevant concepts. The L2 lexicon has strong forward connections to the L1 lexicon. So activation spreads more readily from an L2 word to an L1 word than the other way, because learners practice that direction.","refs":["l2-learning-20","review-53"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"How does the Revised Hierarchical Model explain the Marian and Spivey results?","a":"In the L2 task, Russian and English bilinguals hear “speaker” in English. The concept for speaker partly activates the Russian word for matches, spichki. Russian is the L1, so spichki has a strong conceptual link, and it activates the concept of matches. That conceptual activation draws the eyes to the matches. In the L1 task, English words have weak conceptual links, so they cannot create this interference reliably.","refs":["l2-learning-21","review-54"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"What counter-intuitive prediction does the model make for balanced bilinguals?","a":"It predicts that balanced bilinguals are not two monolinguals in one. Their conceptual links are similarly strong for both languages, so both languages compete and inhibition is engaged all the time. This led to much work on bilinguals’ advantages in non-linguistic cognitive control tasks.","refs":["l2-learning-22","review-55"],"tier":"On the review deck"}]},{"id":"m2-l1-replacement","title":"Can a second language replace the first?","tier":"Possible","blocks":[{"refs":["l2-learning-23","l2-learning-24","l2-learning-25","l2-learning-26","l2-learning-27","l2-learning-28"],"main":"**Korean adoptees raised in French homes showed no trace of Korean on any test.** Korean has a **three-way stop contrast**, where stops at one place come in three kinds, **plain**, **tense** and **aspirated**. So pul (fire), Pul (horn) and phul (grass) are different words that differ only in the first stop. **Ventureyra and colleagues** found that adoptees were no better than monolingual French speakers at hearing these contrasts, while Korean speakers heard them nearly every time.","sub":["Pallier and colleagues also found adoptees no better at recognizing Korean, choosing Korean translations, or detecting repeated excerpts.","Listening to French activated the classical language network in both groups. Subtracting Korean from French revealed no difference between the groups."],"end":"So when L1 is removed from the environment entirely, L2 can appear to replace it completely."},{"refs":["l2-learning-29","l2-learning-30"],"main":"**Competitive dynamics offer one account of replacement.** L1 can be treated as a set of connections within and between brain regions. If those connections are never activated again, L2 could take them over, much as one eye takes over the other eye’s cortex in amblyopia.","end":"So the open question is whether traces of L1 are left that can be salvaged by later learning."},{"refs":["l2-learning-31","l2-learning-32","l2-learning-33"],"main":"**Terry Au’s group found that childhood overhearers of Spanish kept an advantage in pronunciation but not in grammar.** The students had overheard Spanish often as children but never spoke it. They began Spanish classes at about 14 and were tested after second-year college Spanish. Their VOT and accents were more native-like than those of typical late learners.","sub":["The two groups differ in many ways. Korean is rarely heard in France, but Spanish is common in Los Angeles. Spanish may be a heritage language, and Spanish and English share cognates and a writing system."],"end":"So replacement may not be complete, since residual knowledge can be unmasked by later learning. Languages and learning situations differ in ways that are hard to control in experiments."}],"questions":[{"q":"What did Pallier and colleagues find about Korean adoptees raised in France?","a":"They found no remaining trace of Korean. Adoptees were no better than French speakers at recognizing Korean, picking Korean translations or detecting repeated excerpts. Brain imaging also treated Korean like any foreign language, so L2 appeared to replace L1 completely.","refs":["l2-learning-27","l2-learning-28"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"What did Ventureyra and colleagues find with the Korean stop contrast?","a":"They found that Korean adoptees in France were no better than monolingual French speakers at the Korean stop contrasts. Korean speakers heard these differences nearly every time. Both adoptees and French speakers did well on a vowel contrast, so the problem was specific to the Korean stops.","refs":["l2-learning-25","l2-learning-26"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"What did Terry Au’s group find with childhood overhearers of Spanish?","a":"They found that overhearers had more native-like pronunciation than typical late learners after two years of college Spanish. Their VOT nearly matched native speakers. They had little or no advantage in grammar, so early overhearing left traces mainly in the sound system.","refs":["l2-learning-31"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"Why are the adoptee and overhearer results hard to compare directly?","a":"They are hard to compare because the groups differ in many ways besides early exposure. Korean is rarely heard in France, but Spanish is common in Los Angeles and may be a heritage language. Spanish and English also share cognates and a writing system, which Korean and French do not.","refs":["l2-learning-33","l2-learning-32"],"tier":"Possible"}]},{"id":"m2-l2-sensitive","title":"Sensitive periods for L2, Johnson and Newport and the critiques","tier":"On the review deck","blocks":[{"refs":["l2-sensitive-02","l2-sensitive-03","review-44","review-45"],"main":"**Second language learning is a model system for sensitive period research.** **Lenneberg (1967)** tied the sensitive period to the development of **cerebral dominance**, or language **laterality**. He drew on a rough alignment of brain development with language milestones and on resilience after brain injury or surgery.","sub":["Much L1 evidence comes from idiosyncratic or rare cases.","Many people learn second languages at different ages with limited success. So those limits can be used to reason about the timing of the sensitive period."],"end":"So L2 learners give large, ordinary samples where L1 research has only rare cases."},{"refs":["l2-sensitive-04","l2-sensitive-05","l2-sensitive-06","l2-sensitive-07","review-46","review-47"],"main":"**Johnson and Newport (1989) found that grammar scores fall with age of arrival up to puberty and become low and scattered after it.** They tested 46 native Korean or Chinese speakers who arrived in the United States between ages 3 and 39. An untimed **grammaticality judgment test** (GJT) asked whether recorded sentences were grammatical.","sub":["Items tested rules such as plurals, the progressive -ing, determiners and question word order.","Early learners, up to about age 8, were often near-perfect and native-like.","Late arrivals showed a nearly random distribution of accuracy, unrelated to age of arrival."],"end":"The age effect was not explained by amount of experience, motivation, self-consciousness or American identification."},{"refs":["l2-sensitive-08","l2-sensitive-09","review-48"],"main":"**Johnson and Newport read their data as a critical period that extends to second languages, best fit by the maturational state hypothesis.** The dependence of attainment on age fits a loss of learning ability over development. After the period closes, attainment depends on idiosyncratic learning abilities, so late learners vary widely.","sub":["The **exercise hypothesis** says the early capacity declines only if it is not exercised. If it is exercised, language learning ability stays intact for life.","The **maturational state hypothesis** says the capacity declines with maturation whether or not it was exercised.","All the learners had exercised their capacity on an L1, yet later arrivals did worse. So the data fit the maturational state hypothesis."],"end":"This changes how the L1 data are read, because in cases like Genie both hypotheses predict poor learning."},{"refs":["l2-sensitive-10","l2-sensitive-11"],"main":"**Ellis (2005) argued that an untimed GJT may measure explicit knowledge rather than grammar acquisition.** **Implicit knowledge** is intuitive and procedural. It is used automatically in fluent performance, cannot be put into words, and may be learnable only within the critical period. **Explicit knowledge** is conscious and declarative. It is used with controlled effort, can be put into words, and can be learned at any age.","sub":["Untimed GJT performance was correlated with years of formal L2 education.","Starting age was related to the timed GJT, which limits the use of explicit rules."],"end":"So an age effect on an untimed GJT may partly reflect taught **metalinguistic** knowledge (conscious knowledge about language)."},{"refs":["l2-sensitive-12","l2-sensitive-13","l2-sensitive-14"],"main":"**Transfer and the language pairing change the age pattern.** Not all grammar is equally hard. The progressive -ing and word order are easiest, which matches children’s order of learning. Determiners are hard, and Chinese has no determiners. Questions come late for children but are among the easiest structures for late learners.","sub":["**Birdsong and Molis (2001)** tested Spanish speakers learning English. Early arrivals scored near the top regardless of age, while late arrivals declined steadily with age.","That pattern is almost the reverse of the Chinese and Korean learners."],"end":"So age of arrival effects on the GJT may not reflect maturational constraints alone, because the first language matters."},{"refs":["l2-sensitive-15"],"main":"**Overall, L2 attainment is lower for older learners, but how much lower depends on many factors.** It depends on which constructions are tested, how they are tested and the language pairing. It is hard to separate age from the amount and quality of experience. It is also hard to separate transfer from L2 acquisition, and performance from competence.","end":"So these findings may not even deserve to be called conclusions."}],"questions":[{"q":"Why is L2 learning a useful model system for sensitive period research?","a":"It is useful because much L1 evidence comes from idiosyncratic or rare cases such as Genie or hemispherectomy. Many people learn second languages at different ages, often with limited success. So researchers can study large groups and use the limits of L2 success to reason about timing.","refs":["l2-sensitive-03","review-45"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"What did Johnson and Newport (1989) do, and what patterns did they find?","a":"They gave an untimed grammaticality judgment test to 46 Korean or Chinese speakers who arrived in the United States between ages 3 and 39. Scores fell steadily with age of arrival up to puberty, and the earliest learners were native-like. After puberty, scores were low and nearly random with respect to age of arrival.","refs":["l2-sensitive-04","l2-sensitive-07","review-46","review-47"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"Which version of the critical period hypothesis do Johnson and Newport’s data support, and why?","a":"Their data support the maturational state hypothesis. All of their learners had exercised their language capacity on an L1, yet later arrivals still learned English less well. The exercise hypothesis would predict that an exercised capacity stays intact, so it does not fit.","refs":["l2-sensitive-09","review-48"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"What is the difference between implicit and explicit knowledge, according to Ellis (2005)?","a":"Implicit knowledge is intuitive and procedural, used automatically in fluent speech, and cannot be put into words. Explicit knowledge is conscious and declarative, used with controlled effort, and can be put into words. Implicit knowledge may only be learnable within the critical period, while explicit knowledge can be learned at any age.","refs":["l2-sensitive-10"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"Why might an untimed GJT not measure grammar acquisition?","a":"An untimed GJT lets people take their time and apply conscious rules, so it allows explicit metalinguistic knowledge. Untimed GJT scores were correlated with years of formal L2 education. So an age effect on it may partly reflect taught knowledge rather than acquired grammar.","refs":["l2-sensitive-10","l2-sensitive-11"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"What did Birdsong and Molis (2001) find, and what does it mean for maturational accounts?","a":"They found that Spanish speakers who arrived early scored near the top regardless of age, while late arrivals declined steadily with age. This is almost the reverse of Johnson and Newport’s Chinese and Korean learners. So the age pattern depends on the language pairing, which weakens a purely maturational account.","refs":["l2-sensitive-14"],"tier":"Possible"}]}],"review":{"title":"Midterm 2 review guide","lead":"Every question and prompt on the Midterm II review deck, in deck order, answered in a few sentences. Predicted questions at the end change one thing in a review question.","items":[{"n":1,"source":"Midterm II review p2 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"A speech continuum was built by keeping F1 constant and varying F2 onset frequency linearly from stimulus 1 to 13. Why build it this way?","a":"It was built this way so that adjacent stimuli differ by the same physical amount across the entire continuum. Holding F1 constant leaves F2 onset as the only thing that changes. So if some pairs are easier to tell apart than others, the difference must come from perception and not from the acoustics.","refs":["review-2","speech1-14"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":2,"source":"Midterm II review p3 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"A simple ideal observer model assumes perception is non-categorical. What does it predict for labeling, and why would stimulus 2 be 75 percent /b/ and 25 percent /d/?","a":"It predicts that labeling gets blurry as stimuli move away from the category prototypes. Ambiguity scales with distance from the prototype, so labels change gradually between /b/, /d/ and /g/. Stimulus 2 sits a quarter of the way from the /b/ prototype toward /d/, so it would be labeled /b/ 75 percent of the time.","refs":["review-3","speech1-15"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":3,"source":"Midterm II review p4 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"Under the same linear model, what should discrimination look like, and why is labeling still peaked?","a":"Discrimination should be flat and independent of labeling. Labeling has to be peaked because there are three categories and listeners must choose one label. But discrimination only asks “same” or “different,” so if F2 onset differences were perceived equally everywhere, accuracy would be the same for every pair.","refs":["review-4","speech1-16"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":4,"source":"Midterm II review p5 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"What did Liberman and colleagues (1957) actually find for labeling and discrimination?","a":"They found categorical perception. Labels switched sharply from /b/ to /d/ and from /d/ to /g/ over a step or two. Discrimination peaked at those boundaries and was near chance within categories. So equal physical steps were heard very differently depending on whether they crossed a boundary.","refs":["review-5","speech1-17"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":5,"source":"Midterm II review p6 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"Looks to the other member of a minimal pair rise gradually near the boundary. Why is that hard for a thresholded model to explain?","a":"In a thresholded model, nothing passes to the phoneme level until a threshold is crossed. Every sound labeled /b/ would then send the same finished /b/ to the word level. So looks to the pear should be the same for every /b/. Looks instead rose gradually as VOT neared the boundary, which fits a cascaded model where graded feature activation passes forward.","refs":["review-6","speech2-16"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":6,"source":"Midterm II review p7 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"TRACE also includes feedback from the lexical layer to the phoneme layer. What does that mean, and what motivates it?","a":"It means that active words send activation back down to their own phonemes. So an ambiguous sound can be pushed toward the phoneme that completes a word. Experiments like Ganong’s, where word knowledge shifts phoneme category boundaries, motivate adding this feedback.","refs":["review-7","speech2-17"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":7,"source":"Midterm II review p8 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"Ganong (1980) built word and nonword continua such as corpse and gorpse in 5 ms steps. What does a late correction account predict?","a":"A late correction account predicts the same lexical effect at every step of the continuum. Categorical perception works as usual, and then there is a bias to report having heard a word. So even clear endpoint sounds would be pushed toward the word by the same amount.","refs":["review-8","speech2-19"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":8,"source":"Midterm II review p9 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"What does a boundary shift account predict, and why is the lexical effect strongest near the boundary?","a":"A boundary shift account predicts that the lexical effect is strongest for items closest to the category boundary. Those stimuli are ambiguous, so word knowledge can help resolve which first phoneme was heard. At the endpoints the acoustics already decide the sound, so the effect there is near zero.","refs":["review-9","speech2-20"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":9,"source":"Midterm II review p10 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"Which account did Ganong’s results support, and how did later models explain the effect?","a":"The results were strikingly consistent with the boundary shift account. The lexical effect was small at the endpoints and largest near the boundary. Ganong focused on guessing strategies, but later models emphasized feedback from words to phonemes.","refs":["review-10","speech2-21"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":10,"source":"Midterm II review p11 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"How does a TRACE simulation track the Ganong effect over time for an input that becomes plug?","a":"TRACE tracks phoneme and word activations frame by frame as the input unfolds. The first sound is ambiguous between /b/ and /p/, so both are weakly active. After /l/ and the vowel, plug, blood and blush are all active, and /p/ starts to pull ahead of /b/.","refs":["review-11","speech2-22"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":11,"source":"Midterm II review p12 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"Why does the final /g/ favor plug, and how does that explain word effects on phoneme boundaries?","a":"The final /g/ fits plug and is highly inconsistent with blood and blush, so activity accumulates for plug. Plug then sends feedback down to the phoneme level, giving more activation to /p/, the phoneme consistent with the word. So TRACE explains word effects on category boundaries through top-down processing.","refs":["review-12","speech2-23"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":12,"source":"Midterm II review p13 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"Why must a parser that builds syntactic trees deal with limited memory and time constraints?","a":"It must because the parser runs inside a cognitive system with limited memory and limited time. Words arrive quickly, so the parser cannot wait for the end of the sentence before building structure. It also cannot keep every possible tree in play at once, so it needs strategies for choosing a structure early.","refs":["review-13","sentence-04"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":13,"source":"Midterm II review p14 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"What is minimal attachment, and how does it explain “The horse raced past the barn fell”?","a":"Minimal attachment is a strategy of initially preferring the smaller tree, the one with fewer nodes, to save space. The grammatical reading of the horse sentence has many more nodes than the failed parse. So the parser first treats raced as the main verb, and it must rebuild the tree when fell arrives.","refs":["review-14","sentence-05"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":14,"source":"Midterm II review p15 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"What is late closure, and how does it apply to “The burglar blew open the safe with the dynamite”?","a":"Late closure is a bias to keep the current clause open as long as possible. So “with the dynamite” attaches to the big verb phrase starting with “blew open.” That makes the dynamite the tool used to open the safe.","refs":["review-15","sentence-06"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":15,"source":"Midterm II review p16 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"Why is “The burglar blew open the safe with the jewels” slower to read?","a":"It is slower because the sensible reading needs the unpreferred parse. Late closure first attaches “with the jewels” to the verb phrase, so the reader briefly treats the jewels as the tool. Then the reader retraces and attaches the phrase to “the safe,” which appears as longer reading times and extra eye movements.","refs":["review-16","sentence-07"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":16,"source":"Midterm II review p17 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"What kind of parser does this syntactic explanation of the garden path rest on?","a":"It rests on a parser that deals only with the abstract syntactic structure of the sentence, like the lumber mill in speech perception. The parser wants the simplest tree, through minimal attachment, and gets it partly by leaving clauses open, through late closure. So it handles ambiguity efficiently by ignoring it until it becomes a problem.","refs":["review-17","sentence-08"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":17,"source":"Midterm II review p18 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"In Spivey and colleagues (1995), why did people look at the empty towel when told “Put the apple on the towel in the box”?","a":"They looked at it because minimal attachment makes “the towel” seem like the end of the clause “put the apple on the towel.” So the empty towel briefly seemed to be the destination. People looked at it while planning their movements, before moving the apple to the box.","refs":["review-18","sentence-12"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":18,"source":"Midterm II review p19 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"What happens to looks in the apple task when the display has two possible referents?","a":"With two apples, one on a towel, people mostly look at the apple on the towel and then at the box. Looks to the empty towel become much less common. Their eye movements for the ambiguous instruction then look like those for the unambiguous one, so “on the towel” is taken as saying which apple.","refs":["review-19","sentence-13"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":19,"source":"Midterm II review p20 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"Why do people look at the empty towel less when there are two apples in the display?","a":"With two apples, it becomes felicitous, or natural, for the speaker to say which apple is meant. So listeners take “on the towel” as picking out one apple rather than naming a destination. Only the display changed, so context shaped the parse very early.","refs":["review-20","sentence-14"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":20,"source":"Midterm II review p21 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"Describe the attractive dichotomy between the speech of Wernicke’s aphasia and Broca’s aphasia.","a":"Wernicke’s aphasia produces fluent “word salad” or “Jabberwocky,” often with neologisms, while syntax stays roughly intact. Broca’s aphasia produces telegraphic speech, with trouble in word order and grammatical particles. Important content words are relatively spared in Broca’s aphasia, so the two look like mirror images.","refs":["review-21","aphasias-05"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":21,"source":"Midterm II review p22 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"Why is it confusing that Broca’s aphasia is described as a problem with grammar?","a":"It is confusing because Broca’s own patients would not necessarily be classified as Broca’s aphasics today. They were arguably unable to produce speech at all, apparently because of motor control problems. Today they would be diagnosed with apraxia of speech, so the grammar label is far from how Broca described them.","refs":["review-22","aphasias-06"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":22,"source":"Midterm II review p23 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"Why was the dichotomy of Wernicke’s aphasia as a lexicon problem and Broca’s aphasia as a grammar problem so attractive?","a":"It was attractive because it lined up with a sharp distinction in late 20th century formal linguistics between the grammar and the lexicon. That distinction began with Chomsky’s account of language learning as a computational problem. It was supported by his evidence that syntax is independent of semantics, as in “Colorless green ideas sleep furiously.”","refs":["review-23","aphasias-07"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":23,"source":"Midterm II review p24 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"Why do we need psycholinguistic tests to observe grammatical difficulties in comprehension?","a":"We need them because conversation is full of redundant cues to what a speaker means. In “The dog bit the man,” a listener can combine DOG, BITE and MAN into the likeliest event. A listener can also rely on canonical order, with the agent first, then the verb, then the undergoer. So only sentences like “The dog that the man is biting is angry” force grammatical analysis.","refs":["review-24","aphasias-12"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":24,"source":"Midterm II review p25 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"What kind of sentences did Caramazza and Zurif build, and why?","a":"They built center-embedded sentences that defeat meaning-based shortcuts, along with control sentences. Reversible sentences, such as “The girl that the boy is tickling is happy,” need grammar to assign the roles. Improbable sentences, such as “The bird that the worm is eating is blue,” push common sense toward the wrong answer.","refs":["review-25","aphasias-13"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":25,"source":"Midterm II review p26 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"What did the sentence-picture matching results reveal for controls, Wernicke’s patients and Broca’s patients?","a":"Healthy controls were about equally good at all sentence types. Wernicke’s patients were poor overall, probably because of their general comprehension deficits. Broca’s patients had particular trouble with who did what to whom when syntax conflicted with common sense. This specific deficit was taken as evidence that Broca’s aphasia affects syntax specifically.","refs":["review-26","aphasias-14"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":26,"source":"Midterm II review p27 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"Dick and colleagues had participants hear four sentence types and indicate which animal was doing the action. What were the four types?","a":"The four types were active, subject cleft, object cleft and passive. In the active “The dog is biting the cat” and the subject cleft “It’s the dog that is biting the cat,” the doer comes first. The receiver comes first in the object cleft “It’s the cat that the dog is biting.” It also comes first in the passive “The cat is bitten by the dog.”","refs":["review-27","aphasias-16"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":27,"source":"Midterm II review p28 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"How did aphasia patients and healthy aging controls do on Dick and colleagues’ task?","a":"The pattern looked much like what Caramazza and Zurif observed. Broca’s patients had particular difficulty with the syntactically complex object clefts and passives. Their performance was overall not so great, though, since they were well below the controls even on simple actives.","refs":["review-28","aphasias-17"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":28,"source":"Midterm II review p29 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"What happened when healthy undergraduates did the task under stress, and what does it suggest?","a":"Object cleft and passive sentences were particularly vulnerable to every stress condition. The stress conditions included speeding up the speech and remembering lists of numbers as a secondary task. Since healthy people showed the same pattern, the comprehension deficits in Broca’s aphasia may be a performance issue after all.","refs":["review-29","aphasias-18"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":29,"source":"Midterm II review p30 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"Are Broca’s aphasics’ comprehension deficits language-specific? What did Saygin and colleagues (2004) find?","a":"They do not seem to be purely language-specific. Saygin and colleagues (2004) asked patients to match an action to an object. Broca’s aphasics had difficulty whether the action was given in words or shown by pantomime.","refs":["review-30","aphasias-19"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":30,"source":"Midterm II review p31 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"How did performance on the nonverbal action task relate to brain damage?","a":"Performance on the nonverbal version was significantly correlated with damage to Broca’s area. That task needed no sentence to be produced or parsed. So damage to Broca’s area affects understanding actions, not only syntax.","refs":["review-31","aphasias-20"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":31,"source":"Midterm II review p32 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"How is categorical perception tested in infants?","a":"It is tested with behaviors that change when infants notice something new. In the conditioned head turn procedure, infants learn to turn toward a sound when it changes. In high amplitude sucking, sucking drops as a repeated sound becomes familiar and rises again if the infant notices a change.","refs":["review-32","development1-04"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":32,"source":"Midterm II review p33 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"What did Eimas and colleagues (1971) find?","a":"They found that four-month-olds perceive voice onset time categorically. After getting used to one syllable, infants sucked more when VOT changed by 20 ms across an adult category boundary. They did not recover when VOT changed by the same 20 ms within one category, which looked like the no-change control.","refs":["review-33","development1-05"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":33,"source":"Midterm II review p34 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"Why was the Eimas result a big deal?","a":"It was a big deal because categorical perception was considered the first step in turning speech into a symbolic format. Four-month-olds are really little, with color vision still developing, and they are only starting to make vowel-like sounds. So an apparently language-specific ability this early seemed like evidence for innateness.","refs":["review-34","development1-06"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":34,"source":"Midterm II review p35 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"What is perceptual narrowing, and how is it related to categorical perception?","a":"Perceptual narrowing is the decline in the ability to hear speech contrasts the native language does not use. At around six months, English-learning infants discriminate Hindi and Salish contrasts they have never heard. By 10 to 12 months, very few do, while Hindi-learning and Salish-learning infants keep these contrasts. It relates to categorical perception because a non-native difference comes to fall inside one native category, where differences are hard to hear.","refs":["review-35","development1-07"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":35,"source":"Midterm II review p36 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"What is the very compelling narrative about early speech perception?","a":"The narrative says speech perception depends on categorical perception abilities that are special to the language system and present very early. Children start off as “citizens of the world,” able to hear all contrasts because of innate, language-general knowledge. Learning then selects among these innate contrasts and narrows the inventory to the ones meaningful in the native language.","refs":["review-36","development1-08"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":36,"source":"Midterm II review p37 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"What is the inductive problem of early word learning?","a":"The inductive problem is that one naming event fits many possible meanings. A child hearing “cat” could take it to mean cat, furry, pet, whiskers, walking, leg, Felix, animal or alive. This is Quine’s “gavagai” problem, so children need ways to narrow the hypothesis space.","refs":["review-37","development2-12"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":37,"source":"Midterm II review p38 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"What is joint attention, and why does gaze following matter for word learning?","a":"Joint attention is sharing an attentional focus with another person. Gaze following emerges around 9 months of age. It signals an understanding of reference, so the child can tie a word to what the speaker is attending to.","refs":["review-38","development2-13"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":38,"source":"Midterm II review p39 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"What did Baldwin and colleagues find about joint attention and word learning?","a":"They found that joint attention optimizes word learning. Eighteen-month-olds learned a novel word when an adult interacting with them labeled the object they were playing with. They did not learn it when a voice from behind a screen labeled the same object.","refs":["review-39","development2-14"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":39,"source":"Midterm II review p40 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"How did Baldwin use follow-in and discrepant labeling to study intentional cues?","a":"Baldwin taught new words to 16 to 17 and 18 to 19 month olds in two conditions. In follow-in labeling, the adult looked at the toy the infant was focused on. In discrepant labeling, the adult looked at a toy in a bucket. So the design asks whether infants use the speaker’s eye gaze to narrow the hypothesis space.","refs":["review-40","development2-15"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":40,"source":"Midterm II review p41 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"What did Akhtar and colleagues find?","a":"They found that infants used adults’ facial expressions to identify the intended referent of a new word. Facial expressions are affective cues to the speaker’s intention. So intention is conveyed through more than eye gaze.","refs":["review-41","development2-17"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":41,"source":"Midterm II review p42 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"What are word learning strategies, and what is the whole object assumption?","a":"Word learning strategies are default strategies that children bring to word learning, and they narrow the hypothesis space for new words. The whole object assumption is one of them. A child expects a new word to name a whole object rather than one of its parts or properties.","refs":["review-42","development2-19"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":42,"source":"Midterm II review p43 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"What is mutual exclusivity (Markman, 1989)?","a":"Mutual exclusivity is the default assumption that an object can have only one name. The implication is that new words map to nameless objects. This strategy emerges during the second half of the second year.","refs":["review-43","development2-20"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":43,"source":"Midterm II review p44 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"What was Lenneberg’s (1967) theory of the sensitive period, and what motivated it?","a":"Lenneberg proposed that the sensitive period depended on the development of cerebral dominance, or changes in language laterality. One motivation was a rough alignment between brain development and language milestones. The other was a review of resilience after brain injury or surgery, where children recover language and adults do not.","refs":["review-44","l2-sensitive-02"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":44,"source":"Midterm II review p45 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"Why is L2 learning used as a model system for sensitive period research?","a":"It is used because much of what is known about L1 sensitive periods comes from idiosyncratic or rare cases. Many people learn second languages at different times in their lives, often with limited success. So the limits of L2 learning can be used to reason about the timing of the sensitive or critical period.","refs":["review-45","l2-sensitive-03"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":45,"source":"Midterm II review p46 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"What did Johnson and Newport (1989) test, and what did they conclude?","a":"They tested whether a critical period extends to second language learning. They gave a grammaticality judgment test to 46 native Korean or Chinese speakers who arrived in the United States between ages 3 and 39. Earlier arrivals had a clear advantage, so they concluded that the critical period extends its effects to second languages.","refs":["review-46","l2-sensitive-04"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":46,"source":"Midterm II review p47 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"What patterns appear in Johnson and Newport’s data?","a":"There is a strong relationship between age of arrival and accuracy. Early learners, up to about age 8, are often near-perfect and native-like. Late arrivals show a nearly random distribution of accuracy, so age of arrival barely predicts their scores.","refs":["review-47","l2-sensitive-07"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":47,"source":"Midterm II review p48 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"Which version of the critical period hypothesis do Johnson and Newport see as more consistent with their data? Does this change how the L1 critical period data are read?","a":"They see the maturational state hypothesis as more consistent. Their learners had all exercised the capacity on a first language, yet later arrivals still learned English less well. It does change the L1 data, because in deprivation cases like Genie the capacity was never exercised, so both hypotheses predict the same poor outcome.","refs":["review-48","l2-sensitive-09"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":48,"source":"Midterm II review p49 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"What is the cohort effect found by Allopenna and colleagues (1994)?","a":"The cohort effect is early competition from words that share their beginning with the spoken word. When people heard “beaker,” they looked at the beetle about as much as the beaker at first. Looks to the beetle fell once the sounds stopped matching, while the rhyme competitor speaker drew later and smaller looks.","refs":["review-49","l2-learning-16"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":49,"source":"Midterm II review p50 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"What did Marian and Spivey hypothesize about cross-language competitors?","a":"They hypothesized that if L1 words are always activated, Russian speakers should look at a book of matches when the English target is “speaker.” The Russian word for matches, spichki, begins like speaker. So looks to the matches would mean the Russian word was active during an English task.","refs":["review-50","l2-learning-17"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":50,"source":"Midterm II review p51 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"What happened during L2 spoken word recognition in Marian and Spivey’s study?","a":"When only one competitor was present, participants looked at within-language and between-language competitors to the same extent. When both were present, they looked at the within-language competitor more. They still looked at the between-language competitor about twice as often as a control, so L1 words were active during L2 listening.","refs":["review-51","l2-learning-18"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":51,"source":"Midterm II review p52 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"What happened during L1 spoken word recognition?","a":"English words did not seem to be activated when native Russian speakers did the task in Russian. The small difference between the between-language and control conditions was not statistically significant. So the interference runs from L1 into L2 but not the other way.","refs":["review-52","l2-learning-19"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":52,"source":"Midterm II review p53 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"What does the Revised Hierarchical Model of Kroll and Stewart assume about the L1 and L2 lexicons?","a":"It assumes the L1 lexicon is larger and more strongly connected to relevant concepts. The L2 lexicon has strong forward connections to the L1 lexicon. So activation spreads more readily from an L2 word to an L1 word than the reverse, because that direction is emphasized in learning.","refs":["review-53","l2-learning-20"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":53,"source":"Midterm II review p54 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"How does the Revised Hierarchical Model explain the Marian and Spivey results in L2 and in L1?","a":"In the L2 task, Russian and English bilinguals hear “speaker” in English. The concept for speaker partly activates the Russian word for matches, spichki. Russian is the L1, so spichki has a strong conceptual link, and it activates the concept of matches. That conceptual activation draws the eyes to the matches. In the L1 task, English words have weak conceptual links, so they cannot create this interference reliably.","refs":["review-54","l2-learning-21"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":54,"source":"Midterm II review p55 (Fall 2025 deck and identical 2026 Lecture 20 deck)","q":"What counter-intuitive prediction does the model make for balanced bilinguals?","a":"It predicts that balanced bilinguals do not look like “two monolinguals in one.” Their conceptual links are similarly strong for both languages, so competitive dynamics and inhibition are engaged all the time. This led to much work on bilinguals’ advantages in non-linguistic cognitive control tasks.","refs":["review-55","l2-learning-22"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":55,"source":"Predicted. Changes review deck p33","q":"In the Eimas design, suppose a new group heard a VOT change from +60 to +80 ms. What would their sucking do?","a":"Their sucking would keep falling, with little recovery. This is because +60 and +80 ms fall in the same adult category, which is the 20S condition. A 20 ms change only produced recovery when it crossed the boundary, as in the change from +20 to +40 ms.","refs":["speech1-25","review-33"],"kind":"Predicted"},{"n":56,"source":"Predicted. Changes review deck p35","q":"How would Hindi-learning infants at 10 to 12 months do on the Hindi Ta/ta contrast?","a":"They would still discriminate it at the highest level. This is because they hear the contrast in their own language. The decline by 10 to 12 months appears only in infants who do not hear the contrast.","refs":["speech1-26","review-35"],"kind":"Predicted"},{"n":57,"source":"Predicted. Changes review deck p9","q":"On a Ganong continuum, where would the lexical effect be smallest under the boundary shift account, and why?","a":"It would be smallest at the two endpoints of the continuum. Those sounds are acoustically clear, so the first phoneme is already decided. Word knowledge only has room to act near the boundary, where the sound is ambiguous.","refs":["speech2-20","review-9"],"kind":"Predicted"},{"n":58,"source":"Predicted. Changes review deck p12","q":"In the TRACE simulation, what would happen if the final sound were /d/ instead of /g/?","a":"TRACE would favor blood instead of plug. The /d/ fits blood and is inconsistent with plug, so activity would accumulate for blood. Blood would then feed activation back to /b/, so the ambiguous first sound would be heard as /b/.","refs":["speech2-23","review-12"],"kind":"Predicted"},{"n":59,"source":"Predicted. Changes review deck p18","q":"With one apple, what would happen to looks if the instruction were “Put the apple that’s on the towel in the box”?","a":"People would look from the apple straight to the box. The words “that’s on the towel” make the phrase describe the apple, so it cannot be read as a destination. So looks to the empty towel would drop, as in the two-apple display.","refs":["sentence-12","review-18"],"kind":"Predicted"},{"n":60,"source":"Predicted. Changes review deck p26","q":"A Broca’s patient hears “The horse that the bear is kicking is brown.” How would that compare with “The apple that the boy is eating is red”?","a":"The patient would do much worse on the horse sentence. Both animals could sensibly kick, so only grammar reveals who does what. In the apple sentence, apples cannot eat boys, so meaning gives the answer without syntax.","refs":["aphasias-13","aphasias-14","review-26"],"kind":"Predicted"},{"n":61,"source":"Predicted. Changes review deck p29","q":"If undergraduates heard sped-up, filtered speech, which sentence types would they still get right?","a":"They would still be accurate on active and subject cleft sentences. Those types put the doer first, so they need fewer processing resources. Object clefts and passives would drop, because they are the most vulnerable to any stress.","refs":["aphasias-18","review-29"],"kind":"Predicted"},{"n":62,"source":"Predicted. Changes review deck p47","q":"If Johnson and Newport had tested Spanish speakers learning English, would the same age pattern appear?","a":"Birdsong and Molis (2001) found that it would not. Spanish speakers who arrived early scored near the top regardless of age, and late arrivals declined steadily with age. Spanish is closer to English, so transfer changes the age pattern.","refs":["l2-sensitive-14","review-47"],"kind":"Predicted"},{"n":63,"source":"Predicted. Changes review deck p52 and p55","q":"Under the Revised Hierarchical Model, would a balanced Russian and English bilingual look at an English competitor during a Russian task?","a":"The model predicts that they would. A balanced bilingual has similarly strong conceptual links for both languages. So an English word could activate its concept strongly enough to draw looks, and competition would run in both directions.","refs":["l2-learning-22","review-55"],"kind":"Predicted"},{"n":64,"source":"Predicted. Changes review deck p43","q":"Would a 12-month-old be expected to use mutual exclusivity when hearing a new word?","a":"A 12-month-old would not be expected to use it yet. Mutual exclusivity emerges during the second half of the second year. So the assumption that new words map to nameless objects appears only later in the second year.","refs":["development2-20","review-43"],"kind":"Predicted"}]},"experiments":[{"who":"Liberman and colleagues (1957)","title":"Labeling and discriminating a /b/ to /d/ to /g/ continuum","asked":"Are speech sounds heard in proportion to their acoustic differences, or by category?","did":"They used the pattern playback synthesizer to make syllables whose F2 transition changed in equal steps. Listeners labeled each sound as /b/, /d/ or /g/ and judged whether pairs were the same or different.","found":"Labels switched sharply over a step or two. Discrimination **peaked** at the category boundaries and was near chance inside categories.","means":"Equal physical steps are heard very differently depending on whether they cross a boundary. This is **categorical perception**.","refs":["speech1-13","speech1-14","speech1-17","review-5"]},{"who":"Eimas and colleagues (1971)","title":"Four-month-olds and voice onset time","asked":"Do infants hear voice onset time categorically before they can talk?","did":"Four-month-olds heard one syllable through high amplitude sucking until sucking dropped. In 20D, VOT then changed by 20 ms across an adult boundary. In 20S, it changed by 20 ms within one category, and a control group heard no change.","found":"Sucking **recovered** after the 20D change. It kept falling after the 20S change, much like the control.","means":"Infants perceive VOT categorically very early. That seemed like evidence that a language-specific ability is innate.","refs":["speech1-25","development1-05","development1-06","review-33"]},{"who":"Werker and Tees (1984)","title":"Losing non-native contrasts in the first year","asked":"How do age and language experience affect infants’ ability to hear contrasts their language does not use?","did":"They tested English-learning infants at three ages from 6 to 12 months. The contrasts were a Hindi Ta/ta contrast and a Salish ki/qi contrast. Hindi-learning and Salish-learning infants were tested too.","found":"Most English-learning infants discriminated both contrasts at 6 to 8 months, and very few did at 10 to 12 months. Infants learning Hindi or Salish kept their own contrast.","means":"Perception narrows toward the native language during the first year. This is **perceptual narrowing**, and it depends on experience.","refs":["speech1-26","development1-07","review-35"]},{"who":"Cutting and Rosner (1974)","title":"Categorical perception of plucked and bowed sounds","asked":"Is categorical perception limited to speech?","did":"Listeners labeled sawtooth sounds that varied in rise time as pluck or bow, and discriminated pairs along the continuum.","found":"The labels switched over a narrow range. Discrimination **peaked** right at that switch.","means":"Categorical perception appears for sounds that are not language, so it is not special to the language system.","refs":["speech1-29","development1-10"]},{"who":"Jusczyk and colleagues (1977)","title":"Infants and non-speech categories","asked":"Do infants also perceive non-speech sounds categorically?","did":"Infants heard a non-speech sound through high amplitude sucking until sucking dropped. The sound then stayed the same, changed within a category, or changed between categories.","found":"Sucking jumped back up only after a change between categories.","means":"Infants respond categorically to non-speech sounds too. So their categorical response to speech may come from general auditory processing.","refs":["speech1-30","development1-11"]},{"who":"Ohlemiller and colleagues (1999)","title":"Chinchillas categorize a speech continuum","asked":"Do animals without language perceive speech sounds categorically, as Kuhl and Miller (1975) had suggested?","did":"Chinchillas were trained with operant conditioning that either the 0 ms lag or the 80 ms lag sound signaled food. They were then tested on sounds along the /ga/ to /ka/ continuum.","found":"The chinchillas switched over a narrow range of VOTs. Their curves had the same steep shape as human curves.","means":"Categorical perception of speech is not limited to humans.","refs":["speech1-31","development1-12"]},{"who":"Narayan and colleagues (2010)","title":"Some contrasts need exposure to become salient","asked":"Are all speech contrasts equally easy for young infants?","did":"They compared infants’ looking on same and change trials for /ma/ versus /na/ and for /na/ versus /ŋa/. They tested English-hearing infants and Filipino-learning infants at different ages.","found":"Four-month-olds noticed /ma/ versus /na/ but not /na/ versus /ŋa/. Filipino-learning infants missed /na/ versus /ŋa/ at 6 to 8 months but detected it by 10 to 12 months.","means":"Not all contrasts are available innately. Exposure is necessary to acquire some of them.","refs":["development1-13","development1-14","speech1-32"]},{"who":"Maye and colleagues (2002)","title":"Learning categories from a distribution","asked":"Do infants learn speech categories from the statistics of what they hear?","did":"Infants listened passively to a [da] to [ta] VOT continuum in either a bimodal distribution, with two peaks, or a unimodal one, with one peak. At test they heard stimuli 3 and 6 in alternating or non-alternating trials.","found":"Only infants exposed to the bimodal distribution looked differently on the two kinds of trials.","means":"Unimodal infants acted as if 3 and 6 were one category. So a short exposure to a distribution shapes speech categories.","refs":["development1-17"]},{"who":"Social learning studies (researchers not named)","title":"Social interaction in learning speech sounds","asked":"Does social interaction matter for learning speech sounds?","did":"Nine-month-old American infants were exposed to Mandarin live, by video or by audio, and then tested on a Mandarin contrast. In another study, mothers of eight-month-olds responded either right after the infant vocalized or on an unrelated schedule.","found":"Only live exposure produced learning of the Mandarin contrast. Contingent responses increased the quantity and complexity of infants’ vocalizations.","means":"Social interaction works much like a reinforcing stimulus. Humans may find it innately rewarding or be innately good at using social cues.","refs":["development1-18"]},{"who":"McMurray, Tanenhaus, Aslin and Spivey (2003)","title":"Gradient competition between bear and pear","asked":"Is phoneme identification for word recognition thresholded or cascaded?","did":"Listeners heard words from a /b/ to /p/ VOT continuum and clicked a bear or pear image in a visual world display. Their eye movements were tracked.","found":"Clicks gave a step-like identification curve. But looks to the competitor rose gradually as VOT neared the boundary, even when the final click was the same.","means":"Ambiguity is gradient, so graded feature activation reaches the word level. This supports **cascaded** processing.","refs":["speech2-10","speech2-11","speech2-12","speech2-13","review-6"]},{"who":"Andruski and colleagues (1994)","title":"Semantic priming from nonwords","asked":"Can partial or imperfect input activate words and their meanings?","did":"Primes had the voicing of their first consonant altered, so “peace” became “beace” and “king” became “ging.” Priming of related targets was measured with lexical decision.","found":"“Beace” primed “war,” and “ging” primed “queen.”","means":"Lexical representations can be activated by incomplete input, which fits cascaded processing.","refs":["speech2-15"]},{"who":"Ganong (1980)","title":"Word knowledge shifts phoneme boundaries","asked":"Is phoneme identification affected by word knowledge?","did":"He built continua in 5 ms steps between a word and a nonword, such as corpse and gorpse. Listeners labeled the first sound.","found":"The lexical effect was small at the endpoints and largest near the category boundary.","means":"Word knowledge shifts the category boundary itself rather than adding a late reporting bias. Later models explain this with **feedback** from words to phonemes.","refs":["speech2-18","speech2-19","speech2-20","speech2-21","review-10"]},{"who":"Allopenna and colleagues (1994)","title":"The cohort effect in spoken word recognition","asked":"How quickly do candidate words become active as a spoken word unfolds?","did":"Listeners followed spoken instructions such as moving the beaker while viewing a beaker, a beetle, a speaker and a carriage. Eye movements were tracked.","found":"Looks to the beaker and the cohort competitor beetle rose together at first. Looks to the rhyme competitor speaker came later and stayed smaller.","means":"Words that match the input so far compete as soon as the word begins. TRACE simulations closely match this time course.","refs":["speech2-27","speech2-28","l2-learning-16","review-49"]},{"who":"Spivey and colleagues (1995)","title":"An apple, a towel and a box","asked":"Can the visual scene change how a spoken sentence is parsed?","did":"People saw a display and heard “Put the apple on the towel in the box.” The display had either one apple, sitting on a towel, or two apples, only one on a towel.","found":"With one apple, people looked at the empty towel as if it were the destination. With two apples, they looked at it much less.","means":"Different parses of the same sentence are favored in different contexts, very early. This is a big problem for purely syntactic accounts.","refs":["sentence-12","sentence-13","sentence-14","sentence-15","review-18","review-20"]},{"who":"Farmer and colleagues (2007)","title":"Mouse paths in the apple and towel task","asked":"Does syntactic ambiguity affect the action itself, not just eye movements?","did":"Participants carried out the apple and towel instructions with a computer mouse while its path was tracked.","found":"With one apple, the hand curved toward the empty towel on ambiguous instructions. With two apples, ambiguous and unambiguous paths were nearly the same.","means":"Syntactic ambiguity and visual context influence the actual execution of arm movements.","refs":["sentence-16"]},{"who":"Dikker, Rabagliati, Farmer and Pylkkänen (2010)","title":"Early visual responses and form typicality","asked":"Is the earliest response to a word category violation based on abstract syntax or on the word’s physical form?","did":"Readers saw an adjective or an adverb followed by a noun, as in “The beautiful princess” versus “The beautifully princess.” Nouns had a noun suffix, a typical noun form, or neither, as in infant.","found":"The unexpected version produced a larger early visual response only for nouns with a suffix or a noun-like form. Neutral nouns like infant showed no effect.","means":"The earliest syntactic effect comes from visual cortex and depends on predicted word forms.","refs":["sentence-25","sentence-29"]},{"who":"Caramazza and Zurif (1976)","title":"Broca’s patients and reversible sentences","asked":"Do Broca’s patients have a comprehension deficit for syntax, or only a motor problem in speaking?","did":"Patients and controls did a sentence-picture matching task with control, semantically constrained, reversible and improbable center-embedded sentences.","found":"Controls did well on all types, and Wernicke’s patients were poor overall. Broca’s patients failed mainly when syntax conflicted with common sense.","means":"This specific deficit was taken as evidence that Broca’s aphasia affects syntax specifically.","refs":["aphasias-11","aphasias-13","aphasias-14","review-25","review-26"]},{"who":"Dick and colleagues","title":"Sentence types, aphasia and stressed listeners","asked":"Is the Broca’s pattern a loss of grammar or a processing limit?","did":"Participants heard active, subject cleft, object cleft and passive sentences and indicated which animal did the action. Aphasic patients, elderly controls and stressed undergraduates were tested.","found":"Broca’s patients had particular difficulty with object clefts and passives and were weak overall. Undergraduates under speeded speech or memory load showed the same vulnerability.","means":"The comprehension deficit in Broca’s aphasia may be a performance issue after all.","refs":["aphasias-16","aphasias-17","aphasias-18","review-27","review-28","review-29"]},{"who":"Saygin and colleagues (2004)","title":"Actions and sounds without words","asked":"Are aphasic comprehension deficits specific to language?","did":"Patients matched actions to objects, given in words or by pantomime. They also matched environmental sounds or spoken phrases to pictures.","found":"Broca’s aphasics had difficulty with actions whether or not the stimuli were linguistic, and this correlated with damage to Broca’s area. Wernicke’s patients were particularly bad at sound matching in both versions.","means":"Damage to these areas affects meaning beyond language, so the deficits are not purely language-specific.","refs":["aphasias-19","aphasias-20","aphasias-21","aphasias-22","review-30","review-31"]},{"who":"Bates, Wilson, Saygin, Dick, Sereno, Knight and Dronkers","title":"Voxel-based lesion-symptom mapping","asked":"Across many patients, which brain regions go with fluency and comprehension deficits?","did":"They compared symptom scores at every voxel between patients with and without damage there, using MRI data.","found":"Fluency deficits were linked to anterior regions. Comprehension deficits were linked to posterior regions.","means":"Data from many patients fit a rough double dissociation between production and perception.","refs":["aphasias-25"]},{"who":"Jusczyk and colleagues (1999)","title":"Stress as a cue to word beginnings","asked":"Can infants use stress to find words in fluent speech?","did":"Infants aged 7.5 months were familiarized with “kingdom” or “device” and then heard passages containing each word.","found":"Infants familiarized with kingdom preferred the kingdom passage. Infants familiarized with device did not show that preference.","means":"Most English words are stressed on the first syllable, so stress may help signal the beginnings of words.","refs":["development2-03"]},{"who":"Saffran and colleagues (1996)","title":"Finding words by transitional probability","asked":"Can infants find words using only transitional probabilities between syllables?","did":"Infants heard a continuous, monotone stream of CV syllables made of three-syllable words such as tokibu. At test, words were compared with part-words matched for frequency.","found":"Infants looked longer at part-words, a novelty effect.","means":"Infants learned the statistical regularities of the stream, not just how often sequences occurred.","refs":["development2-06","development2-09","development2-10"]},{"who":"Baldwin and colleagues","title":"Joint attention and new words","asked":"Does joint attention help infants learn word meanings?","did":"An adult interacting with 18-month-olds labeled the object they were playing with, or a voice from behind a screen gave the label.","found":"Infants learned the new word in the joint attention group but not in the no joint attention group.","means":"Hearing a word near an object is not enough. Infants need evidence of the speaker’s intended referent.","refs":["development2-14","review-39"]},{"who":"Baldwin","title":"Follow-in and discrepant labeling","asked":"Do infants use the speaker’s eye gaze to decide what a new word means?","did":"Infants aged 16 to 17 and 18 to 19 months were taught new words. In follow-in labeling, the adult looked at the toy the infant was focused on. In discrepant labeling, the adult looked at a toy in a bucket.","found":"The two conditions pulled the infant’s focus apart from the speaker’s focus.","means":"Gaze direction is an intentional cue that can narrow the hypothesis space for new words.","refs":["development2-15","development2-16","review-40"]},{"who":"Akhtar and colleagues","title":"Facial expressions and word reference","asked":"Do infants use emotional cues to find the referent of a new word?","did":"Adults reacted with different facial expressions to objects while a new word was used.","found":"Infants used the adults’ facial expressions to identify the intended referent.","means":"Intention is conveyed through affective and behavioral cues, not only gaze.","refs":["development2-17","development2-18","review-41"]},{"who":"Gertner and colleagues (2006)","title":"Two-year-olds and a made-up verb","asked":"Have young children learned the transitive structure itself?","did":"Two-year-olds heard “The duck is gorping the bunny” while viewing a duck acting on a bunny and a bunny acting on a duck.","found":"Children looked significantly longer at the duck acting on the bunny.","means":"Children who say only short utterances can use transitive word order with a new verb.","refs":["development2-24"]},{"who":"Levi and Polat (1996)","title":"Adult plasticity in amblyopia","asked":"Is plasticity in the visual system gone after the critical period?","did":"Adults with amblyopia, some in their 50s, trained intensively on psychophysical tasks with the affected eye.","found":"Their task performance and general visual function improved.","means":"Plasticity is not abolished in adulthood, so amblyopia fits a sensitive period.","refs":["l1-critical-06"]},{"who":"Li, Ngo, Nguyen, Lam and Levi (2008)","title":"Video games and adult amblyopia","asked":"Can an engaging visual task improve vision in adult amblyopia?","did":"Adults with amblyopia played action video games with the amblyopic eye.","found":"Visual acuity and visual attention improved.","means":"The right kind of experience can reverse deficits late into adulthood.","refs":["l1-critical-07"]},{"who":"Curtiss and colleagues (1974)","title":"Genie","asked":"What happens when a first language is learned only after puberty?","did":"They studied Genie, isolated from 20 months of age and punished for making sounds until she was found at 13. Her language was observed for four years.","found":"She came to understand hundreds of words, some morphology and some prepositions. Her production lagged far behind, and her grammar grew much more slowly than her vocabulary.","means":"The gap between vocabulary and grammar is widely read as evidence that grammar learning is impaired after a sensitive period.","refs":["l1-critical-14","l1-critical-15","l1-critical-16","l1-critical-17"]},{"who":"Mayberry, Lock and Kazmi (2002)","title":"Late first language in deaf children","asked":"Does the timing of a first language matter when upbringing is otherwise typical?","did":"They studied deaf people who first learned ASL at ages 9 to 15 as their first language. They compared them with deaf people who had learned a spoken language before going deaf.","found":"Those with no early language had much more difficulty, and the difference persisted into adulthood.","means":"Having an early language, of any kind, matters for later learning, and deprivation does not extend the critical period.","refs":["l1-critical-18","l1-critical-19"]},{"who":"De Groot and Keijzer (2000)","title":"Cognates in paired-associate learning","asked":"Do people use cognates when learning second language words?","did":"Dutch speakers learned made-up words paired with Dutch words that varied in cognate status and concreteness. They were tested productively and receptively and again a week later.","found":"Cognates were learned more rapidly and remembered more easily. The advantage held in productive testing.","means":"Cognate status contributes to the very earliest stages of second language word learning.","refs":["l2-learning-10","l2-learning-11","l2-learning-12","l2-learning-13"]},{"who":"Marian and Spivey (2003)","title":"Cross-language competitors","asked":"Are similar L1 words always activated when bilinguals process L2?","did":"Russian and English bilinguals followed spoken instructions in English, then in Russian, while viewing objects. Some objects had names in the other language that began like the target, such as matches, spichki, for speaker.","found":"In English, the Russian competitor drew looks about twice as often as a control. In Russian, the English competitor did not reliably draw looks.","means":"L1 words are active during L2 listening, but not the reverse. The competition is asymmetric.","refs":["l2-learning-14","l2-learning-17","l2-learning-18","l2-learning-19","review-50","review-51","review-52"]},{"who":"Ventureyra and colleagues","title":"Korean adoptees and the Korean stop contrast","asked":"Do adults adopted from Korea keep any ability to hear Korean stop contrasts?","did":"Korean adoptees in France, French speakers and Korean speakers judged pairs of Korean stops, vowels and identical sounds.","found":"Adoptees were no better than French speakers on the Korean stop contrasts, while all groups did well on vowels.","means":"The early exposure to Korean left no detectable advantage in hearing its sounds.","refs":["l2-learning-25","l2-learning-26"]},{"who":"Pallier and colleagues","title":"Can a second language replace the first?","asked":"Do Korean adoptees raised in French homes keep any trace of Korean?","did":"Adults adopted at 3 to 8 years were tested on recognizing Korean, choosing Korean translations and detecting repeated excerpts. Their brain responses to French and to foreign languages were also imaged.","found":"Adoptees were no better than French speakers on any task. Their brains treated Korean like other foreign languages.","means":"When L1 is removed entirely, L2 can appear to replace it completely.","refs":["l2-learning-24","l2-learning-27","l2-learning-28"]},{"who":"Au, Knightly, Jun and Oh","title":"Childhood overhearers of Spanish","asked":"Does overhearing a language in childhood leave traces that later learning can unmask?","did":"Students who overheard Spanish as children but never spoke it took Spanish from about age 14. They were compared with native speakers and typical late learners after second-year college Spanish.","found":"Overhearers had more native-like VOT and accents than late learners. They had little or no advantage in grammar.","means":"Replacement of an early language may not be complete, at least for pronunciation.","refs":["l2-learning-31","l2-learning-32"]},{"who":"Johnson and Newport (1989)","title":"Age of arrival and English grammar","asked":"Does the critical period extend to second language learning?","did":"They gave an untimed grammaticality judgment test to 46 native Korean or Chinese speakers who arrived in the United States between ages 3 and 39.","found":"Scores fell with age of arrival up to puberty, and early learners up to about 8 were native-like. After puberty, scores were low and nearly random with respect to age of arrival.","means":"They argued for a critical period that extends to L2, best fit by the **maturational state hypothesis**.","refs":["l2-sensitive-04","l2-sensitive-05","l2-sensitive-06","l2-sensitive-07","l2-sensitive-09","review-46","review-47","review-48"]},{"who":"Ellis (2005)","title":"Implicit and explicit knowledge in grammar tests","asked":"Does an untimed grammaticality judgment test measure grammar acquisition or explicit knowledge?","did":"He compared tests that differ in time pressure and in whether they allow metalinguistic knowledge. He related scores to starting age and years of formal instruction.","found":"Untimed GJT scores were correlated with years of formal L2 education. Starting age was related to the timed GJT.","means":"An untimed GJT may measure **explicit** metalinguistic knowledge more than **implicit** grammar.","refs":["l2-sensitive-10","l2-sensitive-11"]},{"who":"Birdsong and Molis (2001)","title":"Changing the language pairing","asked":"Does the age pattern depend on the learners’ first language?","did":"They repeated the Johnson and Newport study with Spanish speakers learning English.","found":"Early arrivals scored near the top regardless of age, and late arrivals declined steadily with age. This is almost the reverse of the Chinese and Korean pattern.","means":"Age of arrival effects on the GJT depend on the language pairing, not only on maturation.","refs":["l2-sensitive-14"]}],"contrasts":[{"title":"Categorical versus continuous perception","sides":[{"name":"Categorical perception","points":["Labels switch sharply over a step or two of the continuum.","Discrimination peaks at the category boundary and is near chance inside a category.","Similarity is defined by linguistic category.","This is what Liberman and colleagues (1957) found."]},{"name":"Continuous, non-categorical perception","points":["Labels change gradually with distance from each prototype.","Discrimination is flat, the same for every equally spaced pair.","Similarity is defined by physical distance.","This is the ideal observer prediction that the data rejected."]}],"why":"The comparison explains why equal acoustic steps can sound very different. It also explains why categorical perception was treated as the step from physical to symbolic.","refs":["speech1-15","speech1-16","speech1-17","review-3","review-4","review-5"]},{"title":"Thresholded versus cascaded processing","sides":[{"name":"Thresholded, the lumber mill","points":["Activity passes forward only after a threshold is crossed.","Categorical perception arises at phoneme identification.","Every /b/ sends the same finished phoneme to the word level.","Competitor looks should not change with distance from the boundary."]},{"name":"Cascaded, the food web","points":["Activity passes forward continuously over time.","Categorical perception may arise later or be an artifact of the task.","A /b/ near the boundary also sends some support for /p/.","Competitor looks should rise as VOT nears the boundary, as McMurray and colleagues (2003) found."]}],"why":"The eye movement data favor the cascade, because graded competition appears even when the final click is the same.","refs":["speech2-04","speech2-05","speech2-06","speech2-08","speech2-12","speech2-16","review-6"]},{"title":"Late correction versus boundary shift for the Ganong effect","sides":[{"name":"Late correction","points":["Categorical perception works as usual, then a bias favors reporting a word.","The lexical effect is the same size at every step.","Word knowledge acts after perception."]},{"name":"Boundary shift","points":["Word knowledge moves the category boundary itself.","The lexical effect peaks near the boundary and is near zero at the endpoints.","Word knowledge acts during perception, through feedback in TRACE."]}],"why":"Ganong’s lexical effect peaked near the boundary, so the results fit the boundary shift account.","refs":["speech2-19","speech2-20","speech2-21","review-8","review-9","review-10"]},{"title":"Autonomous syntactic parsing versus early interaction","sides":[{"name":"Autonomous syntactic parser","points":["The parser uses only abstract syntactic structure.","It follows minimal attachment and late closure.","Garden paths come from fixed structural preferences.","Context can only repair the parse afterward."]},{"name":"Early interaction","points":["Meaning, word statistics and context work with syntax from the start.","Preferences depend on words, as with the burglar and the policeman.","Many reduced relatives are easy, so structure alone does not predict difficulty.","Context changes the first parse, as with one apple versus two."]}],"why":"Both views explain garden paths, but only early interaction explains why the same structure is sometimes easy and sometimes hard.","refs":["sentence-08","sentence-09","sentence-11","sentence-15","review-17","review-20"]},{"title":"Broca’s aphasia versus Wernicke’s aphasia","sides":[{"name":"Broca’s aphasia","points":["Telegraphic speech with word-finding pauses.","Grammatical morphemes and word order are disturbed, and content words are relatively spared.","Comprehension seems good in conversation but fails when syntax conflicts with common sense.","Described as a problem with grammar, and linked to anterior damage and production."]},{"name":"Wernicke’s aphasia","points":["Fluent speech with good intonation.","Word salad with neologisms, while syntax is roughly intact.","Comprehension is poor overall, even on simple sentences.","Described as a problem with activation in the lexicon, and linked to posterior damage and perception."]}],"why":"The split looked like a grammar and lexicon double dissociation. Later work with stressed listeners and nonverbal tasks weakened the claim that the deficits are purely linguistic.","refs":["aphasias-04","aphasias-05","aphasias-06","aphasias-14","aphasias-25","review-21","review-22"]},{"title":"Competence versus performance in Broca’s aphasia","sides":[{"name":"Competence account","points":["Broca’s patients have lost grammatical knowledge.","Caramazza and Zurif (1976) found trouble when only syntax gives the answer.","The deficit should be specific to syntax."]},{"name":"Performance account","points":["Broca’s patients keep grammatical knowledge but lack resources to use it.","Stressed undergraduates showed the same trouble with object clefts and passives.","Saygin and colleagues (2004) found deficits in nonverbal action tasks too."]}],"why":"The same sentence pattern can come from lost knowledge or from reduced processing resources, so the healthy-listener data matter.","refs":["aphasias-11","aphasias-14","aphasias-18","aphasias-19","review-26","review-29","review-30"]},{"title":"Nativist versus emergentist accounts of speech categories","sides":[{"name":"Nativist","points":["Children have innate knowledge of what language is like.","Infants start as citizens of the world, able to hear all contrasts.","Learning only selects and narrows innate contrasts.","Categorical perception is special to language and to humans."]},{"name":"Emergentist","points":["Associative learning picks up statistical regularities in the input.","Some contrasts start hard and need exposure, as Narayan and colleagues (2010) found.","Learning builds categories from distributions, as Maye and colleagues (2002) found.","Categorical perception appears for non-speech and in chinchillas."]}],"why":"The findings that categorical perception is not limited to language or humans, and that some contrasts must be learned, favor learning within constraints.","refs":["development1-02","development1-08","development1-09","development1-15","development1-17","review-36"]},{"title":"Critical period versus sensitive period","sides":[{"name":"Critical period","points":["Discrete, programmed onset and offset.","No learning before or after the window.","Any late improvement counts against it."]},{"name":"Sensitive period","points":["Gradual onset and offset that experience can shift.","Learning is possible throughout life but best during the window.","Late improvement is harder but possible, as with adult amblyopia training."]}],"why":"Amblyopia and most language evidence fit a sensitive period better, because late learning is reduced rather than impossible.","refs":["l1-critical-02","l1-critical-06","l1-critical-08","l1-critical-19"]},{"title":"Exercise hypothesis versus maturational state hypothesis","sides":[{"name":"Exercise hypothesis","points":["Early in life, humans have a superior capacity for acquiring languages.","The capacity declines only if it is not exercised.","An adult who learned an L1 normally should learn an L2 as well as a child."]},{"name":"Maturational state hypothesis","points":["Early in life, humans have a superior capacity for acquiring languages.","The capacity declines with maturation whether or not it is exercised.","An adult with a normal L1 should still learn an L2 less well."]}],"why":"Johnson and Newport’s learners all had a normal L1 yet later arrivals did worse, so the data fit the maturational state hypothesis. Only L2 data can separate the two.","refs":["l2-sensitive-09","review-48"]},{"title":"Implicit versus explicit knowledge (Ellis, 2005)","sides":[{"name":"Implicit knowledge","points":["Intuitive awareness of linguistic norms.","Procedural, knowing how.","Variable but systematic.","Reached through automatic processing.","Used in fluent performance.","Cannot be put into words.","Possibly learnable only within the critical period."]},{"name":"Explicit knowledge","points":["Conscious awareness of rules.","Declarative, knowing that.","Anomalous and inconsistent.","Reached through effortful controlled processing.","Used when planning or struggling.","Can be put into words.","Learnable at any age."]}],"why":"An untimed GJT allows explicit knowledge, so an age effect on it may not measure the implicit grammar the critical period is about.","refs":["l2-sensitive-10","l2-sensitive-11"]},{"title":"Children versus adults learning a second language","sides":[{"name":"Children","points":["Learn slowly at first, about 20 words by 18 months.","Build concepts and words together.","Often reach native-like ultimate attainment if they start early."]},{"name":"Adults","points":["Learn faster at first, through transfer and better general cognitive skills.","Already have concepts and focus on how the new language expresses them.","Rarely become native-like, partly because of transfer."]}],"why":"Speed of learning and ultimate attainment are separate measures, so children and adults each win on one of them.","refs":["l2-learning-02","l2-learning-03","l2-sensitive-07"]},{"title":"Positive versus negative transfer","sides":[{"name":"Positive transfer","points":["Cognates and loan words make new vocabulary easier.","De Groot and Keijzer (2000) found cognates learned faster and kept longer.","Typologically similar languages benefit more, as with Spanish speakers learning English."]},{"name":"Negative transfer","points":["One L1 category can hide an L2 contrast, as with Hindi dental and retroflex stops.","Classifiers and grammatical gender use cues English speakers learned to ignore.","False friends such as embarazado lead to errors."]}],"why":"The same habit of using L1 knowledge speeds learning where languages match and causes errors where they differ.","refs":["l2-learning-04","l2-learning-05","l2-learning-06","l2-learning-08","l2-learning-09","l2-learning-11","l2-sensitive-12"]},{"title":"Listening in L2 versus listening in L1 (Marian and Spivey)","sides":[{"name":"Task in L2, English","points":["Russian competitors drew looks about twice as often as a control.","L1 words are active during L2 listening.","Strong L1 conceptual links let a partly active Russian word activate its concept."]},{"name":"Task in L1, Russian","points":["English competitors did not reliably draw looks.","L2 words are not reliably active during L1 listening.","Weak L2 conceptual links cannot create this interference reliably."]}],"why":"The Revised Hierarchical Model explains the asymmetry through the difference in conceptual link strength between the two lexicons.","refs":["l2-learning-18","l2-learning-19","l2-learning-21","review-51","review-52","review-54"]}],"hard":[{"title":"Categorical perception does not mean listeners hear nothing inside a category","trap":"Categorical perception means listeners cannot detect any difference between sounds in the same category.","text":"Categorical perception means within-category differences are much harder to hear than equal differences across a boundary. In Liberman and colleagues (1957), within-category discrimination was near chance but real accuracy was a little above what labels predicted. McMurray and colleagues (2003) found that sounds closer to the boundary drew more looks to the competitor word. So listeners keep graded information about how good an example a sound is, even when their final label is the same.","refs":["speech1-17","speech2-12","speech2-25"]},{"title":"A step-like identification curve does not prove thresholded processing","trap":"If listeners’ labels switch sharply, phoneme processing must be thresholded.","text":"A sharp labeling curve only describes the final answer. In McMurray and colleagues’ study, clicks were step-like, but eye movements to the competitor rose gradually near the boundary. A thresholded model sends the same finished phoneme for every /b/, so it cannot produce that gradual change. So the labels can be categorical while the processing behind them is cascaded.","refs":["speech2-11","speech2-12","speech2-16","review-6"]},{"title":"The Ganong effect is a boundary shift, not a general bias","trap":"The Ganong effect just means listeners prefer to report real words everywhere on the continuum.","text":"A general reporting bias would make the lexical effect the same size at every step. Ganong found the effect small at the endpoints and largest near the boundary. Only ambiguous sounds leave room for word knowledge to decide the phoneme. So word knowledge shifts the boundary itself, which TRACE explains with feedback from words to phonemes.","refs":["speech2-19","speech2-20","speech2-21","review-9","review-10"]},{"title":"Broca’s comprehension deficit may not be a loss of grammar","trap":"Caramazza and Zurif proved that Broca’s aphasia destroys grammatical knowledge.","text":"Caramazza and Zurif (1976) found that Broca’s patients fail when syntax conflicts with common sense, and that was taken as evidence for a syntax deficit. Dick and colleagues then found that healthy undergraduates under stress show the same trouble with object clefts and passives. Saygin and colleagues (2004) also found Broca’s patients impaired on nonverbal action matching. So the deficit may be a performance issue, or broader than language, rather than lost grammar.","refs":["aphasias-14","aphasias-18","aphasias-19","review-26","review-29","review-30"]},{"title":"Broca’s own patients were not Broca’s aphasics in the modern sense","trap":"Broca’s original patient Tan is the classic example of agrammatic Broca’s aphasia.","text":"Tan could produce only one syllable but seemed to understand what was said. Broca’s actual patients were arguably unable to produce speech at all, apparently because of motor control problems. Today they would be classified as having apraxia of speech. So the modern description of Broca’s aphasia as a grammar problem is fairly distant from Broca’s cases.","refs":["aphasias-06","review-22"]},{"title":"Infants do not start out able to hear every contrast","trap":"Infants are citizens of the world who can hear all speech contrasts and only lose them.","text":"That narrative fits Werker and Tees (1984), where non-native contrasts fade by 10 to 12 months. But Narayan and colleagues (2010) found that four-month-olds cannot readily hear /na/ versus /ŋa/, whatever their language. Filipino-learning infants only detected it by 10 to 12 months, after exposure. So development also builds some contrasts, and not all contrasts are available innately.","refs":["speech1-26","development1-13","development1-14","development1-09"]},{"title":"Adults are not worse learners in every way","trap":"Children learn a second language faster than adults.","text":"Adults actually learn faster at first, because of transfer from L1 and better general cognitive skills. A child knows only about 20 words after 18 months of exposure. Adults do worse in ultimate attainment, since they rarely become native-like. So speed and final success are separate, and the sensitive period concerns final success.","refs":["l2-learning-02","l2-learning-03"]},{"title":"In the Revised Hierarchical Model, the strong lexical link runs from L2 to L1","trap":"Because L1 is stronger, activation must spread more easily from L1 words to L2 words.","text":"The L1 lexicon is larger and more strongly linked to concepts. But the strong lexical link runs forward from L2 words to L1 words, because that direction is emphasized in learning. Strong L1 conceptual links explain why a partly active Russian word, spichki, could activate the concept of matches and draw the eyes. In the Russian task, English words have weak conceptual links, so they cause no reliable interference.","refs":["l2-learning-20","l2-learning-21","review-53","review-54"]}],"bytopic":{"m2-speech-signal":["speech1-02","speech1-03","speech1-04","speech1-05","speech1-06","speech1-07","speech1-08","speech1-09","speech1-10","speech1-11","speech1-12"],"m2-categorical-perception":["speech1-13","speech1-14","speech1-15","speech1-16","speech1-17","speech1-19","speech1-20","speech1-21","speech1-22"],"m2-infant-speech":["speech1-24","speech1-25","speech1-26","speech1-27","development1-02","development1-03","development1-04","development1-05","development1-06","development1-07","development1-08"],"m2-learning-categories":["speech1-28","speech1-29","speech1-30","speech1-31","speech1-32","development1-09","development1-10","development1-11","development1-12","development1-13","development1-14","development1-15","development1-16","development1-17","development1-18","development1-19"],"m2-cascades":["speech2-02","speech2-03","speech2-04","speech2-05","speech2-06","speech2-07","speech2-08","speech2-09","speech2-10","speech2-11","speech2-12","speech2-13","speech2-14","speech2-15","speech2-16"],"m2-feedback-trace":["speech2-17","speech2-18","speech2-19","speech2-20","speech2-21","speech2-22","speech2-23","speech2-24","speech2-25","speech2-27","speech2-28"],"m2-garden-path":["sentence-02","sentence-03","sentence-04","sentence-05","sentence-06","sentence-07","sentence-08","sentence-09","sentence-10","sentence-11","sentence-12","sentence-13","sentence-14","sentence-15","sentence-16"],"m2-elan":["sentence-18","sentence-19","sentence-20","sentence-21","sentence-22","sentence-23","sentence-24","sentence-25","sentence-26","sentence-27","sentence-28","sentence-29","sentence-30"],"m2-aphasia-models":["aphasias-02","aphasias-03","aphasias-04","aphasias-05","aphasias-06","aphasias-07","aphasias-08","aphasias-09","aphasias-10"],"m2-broca-comprehension":["aphasias-11","aphasias-12","aphasias-13","aphasias-14","aphasias-15","aphasias-16","aphasias-17","aphasias-18","aphasias-19","aphasias-20","aphasias-21","aphasias-22","aphasias-23","aphasias-25"],"m2-words-and-meanings":["development2-02","development2-03","development2-04","development2-05","development2-06","development2-07","development2-08","development2-09","development2-10","development2-11","development2-12","development2-13","development2-14","development2-15","development2-16","development2-17","development2-18","development2-19","development2-20","development2-21","development2-22","development2-23","development2-24"],"m2-l1-critical":["l1-critical-02","l1-critical-03","l1-critical-04","l1-critical-05","l1-critical-06","l1-critical-07","l1-critical-08","l1-critical-09","l1-critical-10","l1-critical-11","l1-critical-12","l1-critical-13","l1-critical-14","l1-critical-15","l1-critical-16","l1-critical-17","l1-critical-18","l1-critical-19"],"m2-transfer":["l2-learning-02","l2-learning-03","l2-learning-04","l2-learning-05","l2-learning-06","l2-learning-07","l2-learning-08","l2-learning-09","l2-learning-10","l2-learning-11","l2-learning-12","l2-learning-13"],"m2-bilingual-competition":["l2-learning-14","l2-learning-15","l2-learning-16","l2-learning-17","l2-learning-18","l2-learning-19","l2-learning-20","l2-learning-21","l2-learning-22"],"m2-l1-replacement":["l2-learning-23","l2-learning-24","l2-learning-25","l2-learning-26","l2-learning-27","l2-learning-28","l2-learning-29","l2-learning-30","l2-learning-31","l2-learning-32","l2-learning-33"],"m2-l2-sensitive":["l2-sensitive-02","l2-sensitive-03","l2-sensitive-04","l2-sensitive-05","l2-sensitive-06","l2-sensitive-07","l2-sensitive-08","l2-sensitive-09","l2-sensitive-10","l2-sensitive-11","l2-sensitive-12","l2-sensitive-13","l2-sensitive-14","l2-sensitive-15"]},"wording":{"speech1-q01":{"college":"Two speakers produce acoustically distinct tokens of the same word, and listeners identify both correctly. Which core problem must a theory of speech perception account for?","simple":"Two people say the same word, but the sounds are physically different. Listeners still hear the same word both times. What puzzle does this show?"},"speech1-q02":{"college":"In a synthesis experiment, vocal-tract resonance is altered while the excitation source is held constant. Which acoustic property is most directly manipulated?","simple":"A computer voice keeps the same buzz (the source) but changes how the mouth shape rings (resonance). What is really being changed?"},"speech1-q03":{"college":"A student interprets each dark horizontal band in a vowel spectrogram as a distinct syllable. Which correction accurately describes what the bands represent?","simple":"A vowel's sound image shows several dark sideways bands. A student says each band is its own syllable. What should the student be told?"},"speech1-q04":{"college":"A synthetic /u/ has low F1 and low F2. If F2 is shifted while the source is held constant, which comparison does this manipulation permit?","simple":"A computer-made /u/ has low F1 and low F2 (the first two resonance bands). If only F2 is moved and the buzz stays the same, what can we learn?"},"speech1-q05":{"college":"Inverse filtering removes the estimated vocal-tract filter and leaves a buzz-like source signal. Why is applying a new filter to that source still informative?","simple":"Taking away the mouth's shaping (the filter) leaves only a buzz. Why is it still useful to add a new filter to that buzz?"},"speech1-q06":{"college":"Tokens of two vowels form overlapping clouds in F1 by F2 space. Which conclusion does this overlap support?","simple":"On a chart of F1 against F2, the dots for two vowels spill into each other. What does that tell us?"},"speech1-q07":{"college":"What methodological advantage do synthesized /b/, /d/, /g/ formant transitions offer over recordings of three distinct natural syllables?","simple":"Why use computer-made /b/, /d/, /g/ sounds instead of just recording three real syllables?"},"speech1-q08":{"college":"Stimuli are spaced in equal F2-onset steps. Pair A straddles the labeling boundary, and pair B falls within one category at the same acoustic separation. What is the purpose of this comparison?","simple":"The sounds change in equal steps of F2 at the start. Pair A sits on two sides of the line between labels. Pair B sits on one side, with the same size gap. Why compare these two pairs?"},"speech1-q09":{"college":"An identification task yields three peaked response regions because participants select among /b/, /d/, and /g/. Why is this result by itself insufficient to demonstrate categorical perception?","simple":"People pick /b/, /d/, or /g/ for each sound, and the answers form three clear humps. Why is that not enough to show categorical perception (hearing sounds in sharp groups)?"},"speech1-q10":{"college":"Suppose a noncategorical listener is uniformly sensitive to equal F2 differences across the continuum. Which discrimination function does this predict?","simple":"Imagine a listener who notices equal F2 changes equally well anywhere along the set of sounds. How well should that listener tell pairs apart?"},"speech1-q11":{"college":"Identification shifts sharply at two locations, and discrimination of equally spaced pairs peaks at those same locations. Which interpretation is best supported?","simple":"Labels flip quickly at two spots, and people tell equal-gap pairs apart best at those same two spots. What is the best reading of this?"},"speech1-q12":{"college":"Two listeners place their category boundaries at different stimulus steps. Which analysis best preserves the theoretical comparison between boundary and discrimination?","simple":"Two listeners put the line between sound groups at different steps. What is the best way to analyze their results?"},"speech1-q13":{"college":"In the shortened class discrimination task, every pair is physically different. Why can a same response nonetheless be informative?","simple":"In the short class test, the two sounds in every pair are really different. Why does it still mean something when someone answers same?"},"speech1-q14":{"college":"How could reduced within-category discrimination be advantageous for speech recognition?","simple":"How could being worse at hearing differences inside one sound group actually help us understand speech?"},"speech1-q15":{"college":"In a high-amplitude sucking paradigm, sucking declines across repeated sounds and then increases after a new token. What does this recovery most directly indicate?","simple":"A baby sucks less as the same sound repeats, then sucks more when a new sound plays. What does that jump back up mainly show?"},"speech1-q16":{"college":"Infants show greater sucking recovery after a change that crosses a category boundary than after an equal-sized within-category change. Which finding does this parallel?","simple":"Babies suck harder again after a sound change that crosses into a new group than after an equal change inside one group. What result does this match?"},"speech1-q17":{"college":"English-learning infants become less accurate over development on a contrast that is used in Hindi but not in English. Which interpretation is consistent with perceptual narrowing?","simple":"English-learning babies get worse with age at hearing a sound difference that Hindi uses but English does not. What does perceptual narrowing (hearing tuned to your own language) say about this?"},"speech1-q18":{"college":"Adults show sharp identification and boundary-aligned discrimination for plucked and bowed nonspeech sounds. Which claim does this result directly challenge?","simple":"Adults sort plucked and bowed sounds (not speech) into sharp groups and hear differences best at the line between them. Which idea does this go against?"},"speech1-q19":{"college":"Trained chinchillas produce categorical-like responses along a VOT continuum of speech sounds. Which conclusion follows?","simple":"Trained chinchillas sort speech sounds along a VOT range (the delay before voicing starts) into groups, much like people do. What does that tell us?"},"speech1-q20":{"college":"A difficult nasal contrast becomes more discriminable with relevant infant experience. How does this finding extend the perceptual narrowing account?","simple":"A hard-to-hear difference between nasal sounds gets easier for babies who hear it in their language. What does this add to the idea of narrowing?"},"speech2-q01":{"college":"Which observation most directly contradicts a model in which lexical processing begins only after phoneme decisions are complete?","simple":"Some models say word recognition starts only after each speech sound is fully decided. Which finding argues most clearly against that?"},"speech2-q02":{"college":"A strict phoneme threshold transmits an identical /b/ output for every token on one side of the boundary. What does this predict about downstream effects of those tokens?","simple":"A strict cutoff sends the same /b/ signal for every sound on one side of the line. What should that mean for the steps that come after?"},"speech2-q03":{"college":"A model continuously transmits partial acoustic activation forward but contains no top-down connections. How should this architecture be classified?","simple":"A model keeps passing partly finished sound information forward, but nothing flows back down. What kind of model is this?"},"speech2-q04":{"college":"Which property of the furniture chain makes it an analogy for thresholded processing?","simple":"What about the furniture chain makes it an example of threshold processing (all-or-nothing passing of information)?"},"speech2-q05":{"college":"A drought immediately affects interconnected levels of a food web while population sizes continue to change. Which processing property does this analogy illustrate?","simple":"A drought right away changes linked levels of a food web, while animal numbers keep shifting. What does this example show about processing?"},"speech2-q06":{"college":"What is the rationale for recording eye movements while participants click on bear or pear?","simple":"Why track where people look while they click on bear or pear?"},"speech2-q07":{"college":"McMurray observes step-like final identification alongside graded competitor fixations. Which conclusion is compatible with both results?","simple":"McMurray finds that final clicks jump sharply from one word to the other, while looks to the other word change slowly. What idea fits both results?"},"speech2-q08":{"college":"Two tokens are both identified as bear, but the token closer to the boundary elicits more fixations on pear. Which variable differs between the tokens?","simple":"People click bear for both sounds. The sound closer to the line gets more looks at pear. What is different between the two sounds?"},"speech2-q09":{"college":"Under a cascaded model, how should competitor fixations change as a token moves toward the category boundary from inside the category?","simple":"In a cascade model (information flows on before it is finished), what should happen to looks at the other word as a sound moves closer to the line?"},"speech2-q10":{"college":"A bilabial stop near the boundary provides partial support for both /b/ and /p/. How can pear draw fixations before the participant clicks bear?","simple":"A lip sound near the line partly fits both /b/ and /p/. How can pear get looks before the person clicks bear?"},"speech2-q11":{"college":"The nonword Beace primes war, and the nonword ging primes queen. What accounts for the effectiveness of these nonword primes?","simple":"Hearing the made-up word Beace speeds up war, and ging speeds up queen. Why do these fake words help at all?"},"speech2-q12":{"college":"Why does categorical identification fail to demonstrate a thresholded mapping from phonemes to words?","simple":"People give sharp, all-or-nothing labels. Why does that not prove that sounds must be fully decided before words are found?"},"speech2-q13":{"college":"Which connection specifically constitutes top-down lexical feedback?","simple":"Which link counts as lexical feedback (a backward link in the model)?"},"speech2-q14":{"college":"What is the purpose of comparing word to nonword continua in which the lexical item favors opposite phoneme interpretations?","simple":"Why compare two sets of sounds where the real word sits at opposite ends?"},"speech2-q15":{"college":"A student claims Ganong simply found a general increase in word responses and disregards where the identification curves diverge. Which evidence does this overlook?","simple":"A student says Ganong just found more word answers overall and ignores where the curves split apart. What is the student missing?"},"speech2-q16":{"college":"A lexically driven boundary-shift account predicts the largest effect at which positions on the continuum?","simple":"If word knowledge moves the line between sounds, where along the set of sounds should the effect be biggest?"},"speech2-q17":{"college":"Ganong's identification curves diverge mainly in the transition region. Which account does this pattern support?","simple":"Ganong's curves split apart mostly in the middle, where the sound changes from one to the other. What idea does that pattern back up?"},"speech2-q18":{"college":"In TRACE, the final /g/ strongly favors plug over blood and blush. Through what mechanism can it influence the ambiguous onset?","simple":"In TRACE, the last sound /g/ strongly favors plug over blood and blush. How can it change how the unclear first sound is heard?"},"speech2-q19":{"college":"Which processing demand distinguishes spoken input in TRACE from a static display of a written word?","simple":"What makes speech input in TRACE harder to handle than a written word that sits still on a page?"},"speech2-q20":{"college":"An onset competitor attracts early fixations that decline as the target's ending arrives. What does this cohort effect demonstrate?","simple":"A word that starts the same way gets early looks, and then fewer looks once the end of the real word is heard. What does this cohort effect show?"},"aphasias-q01":{"college":"A patient comprehends a spoken word but has difficulty repeating it verbatim. Why did disconnection models treat this dissociation as informative?","simple":"A patient understands a heard word but has trouble saying it back word for word. Why did disconnection models find this useful?"},"aphasias-q02":{"college":"Fluent output contains neologisms and paraphasic substitutions, and comprehension is impaired. Which distinction prevents equating fluent speech with intact language?","simple":"A patient talks smoothly but uses made-up words and swapped words, and understands poorly. Why does smooth talk not mean normal language?"},"aphasias-q03":{"college":"A patient omits function words and grammatical particles but retains major content words. What does the term telegraphic characterize?","simple":"A patient leaves out small grammar words but keeps the main content words. What does the word telegraphic describe?"},"aphasias-q04":{"college":"Why is it misleading to treat Broca's original patients as prototypical cases under every later definition of Broca's aphasia?","simple":"Why is it wrong to treat Broca's first patients as perfect examples of every later meaning of Broca's aphasia?"},"aphasias-q05":{"college":"Why are lemma selection and positional-level processing important to the grammar and lexicon proposal?","simple":"Why do lemma selection (picking the word) and the positional step matter for the idea that grammar and words are separate?"},"aphasias-q06":{"college":"Why assess comprehension in a patient whose speech is agrammatic and accompanied by motor impairment?","simple":"A patient speaks without grammar and also has trouble moving the mouth. Why test how well the patient understands?"},"aphasias-q07":{"college":"A patient correctly matches the dog bit the man to the matching drawing. Why does this not demonstrate intact syntactic processing?","simple":"A patient picks the right drawing for the dog bit the man. Why does that not show the patient's grammar is fine?"},"aphasias-q08":{"college":"For the dog that the man is biting is angry, which interpretation depends on computing the sentence's structural relations?","simple":"In the dog that the man is biting is angry, which meaning can only be found by working out the sentence structure?"},"aphasias-q09":{"college":"Why include reversible and implausible center-embedded sentences in addition to semantically constrained ones?","simple":"Why test sentences where either noun could do the action, or where the event is unlikely, along with sentences where meaning gives the answer?"},"aphasias-q10":{"college":"Broca-type patients have particular difficulty when syntax conflicts with probable thematic roles, whereas Wernicke-type patients perform poorly across conditions. What motivated the original inference?","simple":"Broca-type patients struggle most when grammar goes against the likely roles. Wernicke-type patients do poorly on almost everything. What did this first lead researchers to think?"},"aphasias-q11":{"college":"Why replace a broad clinical description such as poor comprehension with controlled task contrasts?","simple":"Why swap a broad label like poor comprehension for careful comparisons between tasks?"},"aphasias-q12":{"college":"For it's the cat that the dog is biting, participants must identify the agent. Which response reflects syntactic analysis rather than a first-noun heuristic?","simple":"For it's the cat that the dog is biting, people must say who is doing the biting. Which answer follows the grammar instead of just picking the first noun?"},"aphasias-q13":{"college":"Object clefts and passives are more difficult than active sentences for patient groups. What must an adequate explanation account for?","simple":"Patient groups find object clefts and passives harder than plain active sentences. What does a good explanation have to cover?"},"aphasias-q14":{"college":"Healthy undergraduates under time pressure or memory load show similar vulnerability to complex sentence types. Which explanation does this support?","simple":"Healthy college students who are rushed or holding things in memory have the same trouble with hard sentence types. Which explanation does this back up?"},"aphasias-q15":{"college":"Saygin compares verbal and nonverbal versions of action and object matching. Why keep the action relation constant while varying its presentation format?","simple":"Saygin has patients match actions and objects using words or using pantomime (acted-out actions). Why keep the same action link but change how it is shown?"},"aphasias-q16":{"college":"Performance on nonverbal action matching correlates with lesions involving Broca's area. Which interpretation is most appropriate?","simple":"Doing poorly on action matching without words goes along with damage to Broca's area. What is the best reading of this?"},"aphasias-q17":{"college":"A patient has difficulty matching both spoken words and meaningful environmental sounds to drawings. Which common processing demand should be considered?","simple":"A patient has trouble matching both spoken words and meaningful everyday sounds to drawings. What job do both tasks share?"},"aphasias-q18":{"college":"Deficits in both verbal and nonverbal sound matching are associated with damage to posterior language regions. Why integrate behavioral and lesion-mapping evidence?","simple":"Trouble matching both words and other sounds goes along with damage toward the back of the language areas. Why put the task results and the brain damage results together?"},"aphasias-q19":{"college":"Which research approach most directly advances the debate between grammar-specific and general-processing accounts?","simple":"Which kind of study helps most in deciding whether the problem is grammar itself or general thinking resources?"},"aphasias-q20":{"college":"Voxel-based lesion mapping associates anterior damage with broad fluency deficits and posterior damage with broad comprehension deficits. Which claim is appropriate to this level of measurement?","simple":"Voxel-based mapping (linking small spots of brain damage to scores) ties front damage to broad fluency problems and back damage to broad understanding problems. What claim fits how rough these measures are?"},"development1-q01":{"college":"Why does the generativity of language pose a problem for a stored-list account of acquisition?","simple":"Why is generativity (making endless new sentences) a problem for the idea that children just store a list of sentences?"},"development1-q02":{"college":"Which finding would most directly undermine the claim that reinforcement of grammatical utterances is sufficient to teach grammar?","simple":"Some say children learn grammar only because adults reward correct sentences. Which finding argues most clearly against that?"},"development1-q03":{"college":"Why did categorical perception initially seem compelling as evidence for a language-specific perceptual mechanism?","simple":"Why did categorical perception (hearing sounds in sharp groups) at first look like proof of a special language system?"},"development1-q04":{"college":"In an infant habituation paradigm, which comparison best isolates discrimination from a general increase in responding?","simple":"In a baby habituation study (the baby gets used to a sound), which comparison best shows the baby noticed a change and is not just getting more active?"},"development1-q05":{"college":"Why equate the acoustic magnitude of within-category and between-category VOT changes?","simple":"Why make the VOT change (delay before voicing) the same size inside a sound group and across two groups?"},"development1-q06":{"college":"Why did Eimas's infant findings encourage nativist interest without, on their own, demonstrating language specificity?","simple":"Why did Eimas's baby results make people think of inborn abilities, yet not prove a special language system?"},"development1-q07":{"college":"Younger English-learning infants discriminate a nonnative contrast better than older English-learning infants. Which additional comparison would best identify language-specific tuning?","simple":"Younger English-learning babies hear a foreign sound difference better than older English-learning babies. What extra group would best show that their own language is doing the tuning?"},"development1-q08":{"college":"What initial perceptual state does a pure selection account, in which infants select from universal contrasts, predict?","simple":"One idea says babies start with all possible sound differences and keep only some. What does it say babies can hear at the start?"},"development1-q09":{"college":"Which pair of findings most directly challenges the claims that categorical perception is exclusive to speech and exclusive to humans?","simple":"Which two findings best argue against the ideas that hearing sounds in sharp groups happens only for speech and only in humans?"},"development1-q10":{"college":"Adults discriminate equal-sized nonspeech differences best across the pluck and bow boundary. Which inference is warranted?","simple":"Adults hear equal-sized differences in sounds that are not speech best at the line between pluck and bow. What can we fairly conclude?"},"development1-q11":{"college":"Infant sucking recovers for between-category nonspeech changes but not for matched within-category changes. What does this comparison indicate?","simple":"Babies suck harder again when a non-speech sound crosses into a new group, but not for an equal change inside one group. What does this show?"},"development1-q12":{"college":"In the chinchilla training procedure, what does the discriminative stimulus (SD) signal to the animal?","simple":"In the chinchilla training, what does the SD (the cue signal) tell the animal?"},"development1-q13":{"college":"Young infants discriminate /ma/ from /na/ but do not discriminate /na/ from /ŋa/ comparably well. Which claim does this weaken?","simple":"Young babies can tell /ma/ from /na/ but have a harder time telling /na/ from /ŋa/. Which idea does this weaken?"},"development1-q14":{"college":"Filipino-learning infants become better at the difficult nasal contrast with age. Which process must be added to a narrowing account based solely on loss?","simple":"Filipino-learning babies get better at the hard nasal sound difference as they grow. What must be added to the idea that babies only lose sound differences?"},"development1-q15":{"college":"In the associative learning example, why can a bell eventually come to influence salivation?","simple":"In the learning-by-pairing example, why can a bell end up causing drooling (salivation)?"},"development1-q16":{"college":"What information does a statistical vowel learner extract from a distribution of tokens in F1 by F2 space?","simple":"A statistical learner hears many vowel sounds spread out on an F1 and F2 chart. What does it pick up from that spread?"},"development1-q17":{"college":"In Maye's study, test tokens 3 and 6 were presented equally often during familiarization in both groups. Why is this design feature important?","simple":"In Maye's study, test sounds 3 and 6 were played the same number of times for both groups. Why does that matter?"},"development1-q18":{"college":"Following bimodal or unimodal familiarization, only one group discriminates alternating from repeated test tokens. Which result is consistent with distributional learning?","simple":"One group heard sounds bunched in two humps (bimodal), the other in one hump (unimodal). Only one group then tells switching sounds from repeated sounds. What result fits learning from how sounds are spread out?"},"development1-q19":{"college":"Live Mandarin exposure yields clearer learning than audio-only or television exposure. Which factor does this comparison emphasize?","simple":"Babies learn Mandarin sounds better from a live person than from audio or TV. What does this comparison point to?"},"development1-q20":{"college":"Which statement best characterizes constrained statistical learning?","simple":"Which statement best describes statistical learning that has limits on what it picks up?"},"development2-q01":{"college":"Why is speech segmentation not equivalent to dividing the signal at silent intervals?","simple":"Why can't we find words in speech just by cutting at the quiet gaps?"},"development2-q02":{"college":"After familiarization, infants recognize kingdom in passages more readily than device. Which segmentation cue does this implicate?","simple":"After hearing both words, babies spot kingdom in a story more easily than device. Which cue for finding words is at work?"},"development2-q03":{"college":"A sound sequence that rarely occurs within words appears in connected speech. How can phonotactic knowledge aid segmentation?","simple":"Two sounds that rarely sit together inside a word show up side by side in speech. How can phonotactic knowledge (knowing which sound orders are allowed) help?"},"development2-q04":{"college":"Why divide the frequency of a syllable pair by the frequency of its first syllable?","simple":"Why compare how often a pair of syllables occurs with how often its first syllable occurs?"},"development2-q05":{"college":"What point about segmentation cues do ice cream and I scream illustrate?","simple":"What do ice cream and I scream show about finding word edges in speech?"},"development2-q06":{"college":"Why did Saffran's experiment use monotone, continuous streams of CV syllables?","simple":"Why did Saffran play flat-sounding, nonstop strings of CV syllables (a consonant then a vowel)?"},"development2-q07":{"college":"The artificial speech stream contains no pauses, yet syllables within words reliably predict one another. What can a statistical learner extract?","simple":"The made-up speech has no gaps, but syllables inside each word always come in the same order. What can a learner who tracks patterns pick up?"},"development2-q08":{"college":"A color-coded printout reveals the artificial words in the stream. Why should the colors not be regarded as a cue available to infants?","simple":"A printout uses colors to mark the made-up words. Why should the colors not count as a cue the babies had?"},"development2-q09":{"college":"A trained word and a part-word spanning a word boundary occur with equal frequency. What is the purpose of comparing them?","simple":"A learned word and a part-word (pieces of two words joined across the edge) were heard the same number of times. Why compare them?"},"development2-q10":{"college":"GOPILA and BU-GIKO each occur twice, but GI follows BU on only some occurrences of BU. Which quantity differs?","simple":"GOPILA and BU-GIKO are each heard twice, but GI comes after BU only some of the times BU is heard. What is different?"},"development2-q11":{"college":"At test, infants attend longer to part-words than to frequency-matched trained words. What does this novelty preference indicate?","simple":"At test, babies listen longer to part-words than to learned words heard just as often. What does this preference for the new-seeming item show?"},"development2-q12":{"college":"Why does hearing cat in the presence of an animal fail to determine the word's meaning uniquely?","simple":"Why does hearing cat near an animal not tell a child exactly what the word means?"},"development2-q13":{"college":"Beyond two people simply viewing the same object, what does joint attention contribute?","simple":"What does joint attention give a child that two people just looking at the same thing does not?"},"development2-q14":{"college":"Infants learn a label produced by an interacting adult but not one produced by a voice behind a screen, even while handling the object. Which simple account is weakened?","simple":"Babies learn a name from an adult who plays with them, but not from a hidden voice behind a screen, even when they hold the toy. Which simple idea does this weaken?"},"development2-q15":{"college":"An adult looks into a bucket while labeling a toy other than the one the infant is holding. What distinguishes using the speaker's intention from a self-focused association?","simple":"An adult looks into a bucket while naming a toy that is not the one the baby is holding. What would show the baby uses the speaker's intention instead of a self-focused association (linking the word to whatever the baby is focused on)?"},"development2-q16":{"college":"In Akhtar's task, the adult's emotional expression helps infants determine a novel word's referent. What inference does this require?","simple":"In Akhtar's task, the adult's facial reaction helps babies work out what a new word names. What kind of reasoning is the baby using?"},"development2-q17":{"college":"A novel noun is used in the presence of an unfamiliar object with an unusual handle. What does the whole-object assumption initially favor?","simple":"A new noun is said near a strange object with an odd handle. What does the whole-object assumption (a default guess children make about new nouns) favor at first?"},"development2-q18":{"college":"A familiar dog and an unfamiliar object are both present when a novel label is requested. Which choice does mutual exclusivity predict?","simple":"A known dog and an unknown object are both there. Someone uses a new word and asks for the thing it names. What does mutual exclusivity (the idea that each thing has only one name) predict?"},"development2-q19":{"college":"Why is the utterance mommy up insufficient on its own to demonstrate an adult-like productive construction?","simple":"Why does a child saying mommy up not prove, by itself, that the child has an adult-like grammar pattern?"},"development2-q20":{"college":"Children match the duck is gorping the bunny to a novel action in which the duck acts on the bunny. Why use a novel verb?","simple":"Children match the duck is gorping the bunny to a new action where the duck does something to the bunny. Why use a made-up verb?"},"l1-critical-q01":{"college":"Adults improve with intensive training, although early learning is easier. Which developmental timing account best accommodates both findings?","simple":"Adults get better with hard training, but learning is easier early in life. Which timing idea fits both facts?"},"l1-critical-q02":{"college":"After a childhood cataract is surgically corrected, vision in the affected eye remains poor. What accounts for the persistent deficit?","simple":"A child's cloudy lens (cataract) is fixed, but that eye still sees poorly. Why does the problem stay?"},"l1-critical-q03":{"college":"In the amblyopia account, what does colonization by the stronger eye mean?","simple":"In the lazy-eye account, what does it mean that the better eye takes over (colonizes)?"},"l1-critical-q04":{"college":"What is the rationale for patching the stronger eye instead of allowing continued reliance on it?","simple":"Why cover the better eye with a patch instead of letting the child keep using it?"},"l1-critical-q05":{"college":"What does perceptual learning in adults with amblyopia indicate about an early optimal window?","simple":"What does practice-based learning in adults with a lazy eye (amblyopia) show about an early best time for learning?"},"l1-critical-q06":{"college":"Which sequence best summarizes the amblyopia example?","simple":"Which order of events best sums up the lazy-eye example?"},"l1-critical-q07":{"college":"In a deaf child's history, why distinguish accessible language input from mere exposure to speech sounds?","simple":"For a deaf child, why does it matter whether language was truly usable, not just whether speech sounds were around?"},"l1-critical-q08":{"college":"What motivated Lenneberg's maturational hypothesis?","simple":"What led Lenneberg to say language learning depends on the brain growing up?"},"l1-critical-q09":{"college":"Why does a large age gap in the postmortem brain data matter?","simple":"Why is a big gap in ages among the brains studied after death a problem?"},"l1-critical-q10":{"college":"Why were outcomes after childhood hemispherectomy relevant to accounts based on cerebral dominance?","simple":"Why did results from children who had half the brain removed (hemispherectomy) matter for ideas about one side of the brain taking over language?"},"l1-critical-q11":{"college":"Children with perinatal stroke outperform comparable adults but may remain in the low-normal range. Which conclusion is consistent with both observations?","simple":"Children who had a stroke around birth do better than adults with similar strokes, but may still score at the low end of normal. What conclusion fits both facts?"},"l1-critical-q12":{"college":"Why analyze Genie's vocabulary and grammar separately instead of reporting a single language score?","simple":"Why look at Genie's words and grammar one at a time instead of giving one overall language score?"},"l1-critical-q13":{"college":"Genie acquired many words and some comprehension of plurals. Which possibility does this rule out?","simple":"Genie learned many words and came to understand some plurals. What does this rule out?"},"l1-critical-q14":{"college":"Why should Genie's production deficit not be taken as a direct index of her vocabulary knowledge?","simple":"Why shouldn't Genie's trouble speaking be read as simply showing how many words she knew?"},"l1-critical-q15":{"college":"Which pattern in Genie's development specifically motivated an interpretation based on the timing of first-language grammar?","simple":"What pattern in Genie's learning made people think grammar in a first language has a time limit?"},"l1-critical-q16":{"college":"Two groups begin ASL late, one with an early spoken first language and one with no accessible early language. Why is this contrast important?","simple":"Two groups start ASL late. One group had a spoken first language early. The other had no usable language early. Why is this comparison important?"},"l1-critical-q17":{"college":"Late learners of sign as a second language outperform late learners of sign as a first language. Which explanation does this support?","simple":"People who learn sign late as a second language do better than people who learn sign late as their first language. Which explanation does this back up?"},"l1-critical-q18":{"college":"If withholding a first language does not preserve child-like acquisition ability later, which simple hypothesis is weakened?","simple":"If keeping a child from any first language does not save a child-like ability to learn later, which simple idea is weakened?"},"l1-critical-q19":{"college":"Which claim best summarizes the first-language evidence?","simple":"Which statement best sums up what the first-language cases show?"},"l1-critical-q20":{"college":"How does adult visual learning refine the discussion of a critical period for language?","simple":"How do results on adults learning to see better sharpen the debate about a critical period (a time limit) for language?"},"sentence-q01":{"college":"For the horse raced past the barn fell, what is the correct structural analysis?","simple":"In the horse raced past the barn fell, what does the sentence actually mean once it is read correctly?"},"sentence-q02":{"college":"Why do garden-path sentences provide evidence for incremental processing?","simple":"How do garden-path sentences (sentences that lead you the wrong way) show that we interpret words as they come?"},"sentence-q03":{"college":"A parser following minimal attachment encounters a locally ambiguous reduced relative clause. Which initial analysis does it prefer?","simple":"A parser that follows minimal attachment (choose the simplest structure) meets a reduced relative that could be read two ways. Which reading does it pick first?"},"sentence-q04":{"college":"In the burglar blew open the safe with the dynamite, what role does the preferred verb-phrase attachment assign to dynamite?","simple":"In the burglar blew open the safe with the dynamite, the favored reading links with the dynamite to the action. What does that make the dynamite?"},"sentence-q05":{"college":"Why is the version ending in with the jewels more difficult in the matching safe sentence?","simple":"Why is the safe sentence that ends with the jewels harder to read?"},"sentence-q06":{"college":"Which finding is most problematic for a strictly syntax-first model of initial parsing?","simple":"Which finding causes the most trouble for the idea that grammar alone sets the first reading of a sentence?"},"sentence-q07":{"college":"Many reduced relative clauses are easy to process when semantics and usage favor them. What does the constraint-based account conclude from this?","simple":"Many reduced relatives are easy to read when meaning and common use point toward them. What does the constraint-based account (many cues work together) conclude?"},"sentence-q08":{"college":"Why might the burglar arrested in the parking lot be easier to process than the matching policeman sentence?","simple":"Why might the burglar arrested in the parking lot be easier to read than the same sentence with policeman?"},"sentence-q09":{"college":"In the one-apple display, why are fixations on the empty towel informative?","simple":"In the scene with one apple, why do looks at the empty towel tell us something?"},"sentence-q10":{"college":"Adding a second apple makes on the towel useful for identifying which apple is meant. Which eye-movement pattern is expected?","simple":"With a second apple added, on the towel now helps tell which apple is meant. What pattern of looking should follow?"},"sentence-q11":{"college":"In the one-apple and two-apple comparison, which factor is held constant, making the contextual effect important?","simple":"In the one-apple and two-apple test, what stays the same that makes the effect of the scene matter?"},"sentence-q12":{"college":"A mouse-tracking trajectory curves toward an incorrect destination before reaching the correct one. What does the trajectory reveal beyond endpoint accuracy?","simple":"A mouse path bends toward a wrong spot before ending at the right one. What does the path show that the final click does not?"},"sentence-q13":{"college":"What does time-locking an ERP to a particular word allow researchers to determine?","simple":"What can researchers learn by lining up the ERP (brain wave recording) to the moment a word appears?"},"sentence-q14":{"college":"Why is eaten disruptive in the pizza was in the eaten?","simple":"Why does eaten cause trouble in the pizza was in the eaten?"},"sentence-q15":{"college":"What does the early latency of the ELAN most directly establish?","simple":"What does the fast timing of the ELAN (an early brain response) most directly show?"},"sentence-q16":{"college":"Through what mechanism can a syntactic expectation influence early visual processing?","simple":"How can expecting a certain kind of word change early seeing of the word?"},"sentence-q17":{"college":"Perches functions as a noun in one sentence and as a verb in another. What makes this useful experimentally?","simple":"Perches is a noun in one sentence and a verb in another. Why is that useful in an experiment?"},"sentence-q18":{"college":"Perches looks relatively noun-like in form but is used as a verb. Which research question follows from form-prediction accounts?","simple":"Perches looks a lot like a noun but is used as a verb. What question do form-prediction accounts (the idea that the brain predicts how the next word will look or sound from its expected category) raise?"},"sentence-q19":{"college":"An early effect occurs for violations with strongly noun-like forms but not for neutral nouns. Which account does this support?","simple":"An early brain effect shows up for very noun-looking words in the wrong spot, but not for plain nouns. Which idea does this back up?"},"sentence-q20":{"college":"Which pairing correctly matches an example sentence with its central lesson?","simple":"Which pair correctly matches an example sentence with its main point?"},"l2-learning-q01":{"college":"An adult acquires initial L2 vocabulary rapidly but does not attain native-like proficiency later. Why are these findings not contradictory?","simple":"An adult learns first words in a second language fast but never ends up sounding native. Why is that not a contradiction?"},"l2-learning-q02":{"college":"Why might older L2 learners benefit from already understanding events and spatial relations?","simple":"Why can it help older second-language learners that they already understand events and where things are?"},"l2-learning-q03":{"college":"Under what condition does reusing an L1 representation constitute negative transfer?","simple":"When does using something from your first language count as negative transfer (carrying over something that hurts)?"},"l2-learning-q04":{"college":"English listeners assimilate Hindi dental and retroflex stops to a single familiar category. Which learning problem results?","simple":"English listeners hear Hindi dental and retroflex stops as one familiar English sound. What learning problem does that cause?"},"l2-learning-q05":{"college":"Why might classifiers pose difficulty for an English-speaking learner of Chinese beyond memorizing a new form?","simple":"Why can classifiers (counting words tied to nouns) be hard for an English speaker learning Chinese, beyond just memorizing a new word?"},"l2-learning-q06":{"college":"Two French synonyms carry different grammatical genders. What must the learner keep distinct?","simple":"Two French words mean the same thing but have different grammatical genders. What does the learner need to keep apart?"},"l2-learning-q07":{"college":"Why can cognates facilitate early L2 learning?","simple":"Why can cognates (words that look and sound alike across languages) help early learning?"},"l2-learning-q08":{"college":"A learner interprets embarazado by way of English embarrassed. Which process produces this error?","simple":"A learner reads embarazado as English embarrassed. What causes this mistake?"},"l2-learning-q09":{"college":"Why use invented, foreign-like forms in a paired-associate learning task?","simple":"In a word-pair learning task, why use made-up words that sound foreign?"},"l2-learning-q10":{"college":"Cognate-like pairs are acquired faster and recalled better a week later. What does this pattern support?","simple":"Pairs that look like cognates are learned faster and remembered better a week later. What does this show?"},"l2-learning-q11":{"college":"Why is producing a new form more diagnostic than only recognizing its familiar meaning?","simple":"Why does making a new word yourself tell us more than just picking out what it means?"},"l2-learning-q12":{"college":"A results table shows both a concreteness advantage and a cognate advantage. Why should these effects be kept distinct?","simple":"A table shows an advantage for concrete words and an advantage for cognates. Why should these not be lumped together?"},"l2-learning-q13":{"college":"What defines an onset cohort competitor?","simple":"What makes a word an onset cohort competitor (one kind of rival word)?"},"l2-learning-q14":{"college":"For Russian and English bilingual listeners, why can matches compete with the English target speaker?","simple":"For people who speak Russian and English, why can matches compete with the English word speaker?"},"l2-learning-q15":{"college":"In the English task, competitors with overlapping Russian names draw fixations above control levels, even alongside an English competitor. What follows?","simple":"In the English task, objects whose Russian names start like the target get more looks than control objects, even with an English rival on screen. What does this show?"},"l2-learning-q16":{"college":"In the Russian task, the English competitor's small advantage is not statistically reliable. How should this result be reported?","simple":"In the Russian task, the English rival gets a small edge, but it is not statistically reliable. How should this be described?"},"l2-learning-q17":{"college":"In the bilingual lexicon model, which configuration of connections accounts for stronger L1 influence during L2 processing?","simple":"In the model of two-language memory, which set of links explains why the first language pulls harder while using the second?"},"l2-learning-q18":{"college":"Why does balanced bilingualism not necessarily imply two separate monolingual systems?","simple":"Why does being equally good in two languages not mean having two separate one-language systems?"},"l2-learning-q19":{"college":"Korean adoptees perform like monolingual French listeners on the tested Korean contrasts. What can be concluded?","simple":"Korean adoptees score like French-only listeners on the Korean sound differences tested. What can we conclude?"},"l2-learning-q20":{"college":"When comparing adoptees with childhood overhearers, why consider differences in ongoing language exposure?","simple":"Why look at how much each group kept hearing the language when comparing adoptees with people who overheard it as children?"},"l2-sensitive-q01":{"college":"According to Lenneberg's cerebral-dominance account, what factor is relevant to a window for language learning?","simple":"In Lenneberg's account of one brain side taking charge, what matters for the time window for learning language?"},"l2-sensitive-q02":{"college":"What makes L2 learners useful for research on age of acquisition?","simple":"Why are second-language learners useful for studying how the age you start affects learning?"},"l2-sensitive-q03":{"college":"In Johnson and Newport's research question, what does ultimate attainment refer to?","simple":"In Johnson and Newport's question, what does ultimate attainment (final skill level) mean?"},"l2-sensitive-q04":{"college":"Participants listen to sentences and make untimed grammaticality judgments. Which type of knowledge might this procedure particularly encourage?","simple":"People hear sentences and decide, with no time limit, whether each is grammatical. What kind of knowledge might this let them lean on?"},"l2-sensitive-q05":{"college":"Why analyze early and late arrival ranges separately instead of reporting a single overall correlation?","simple":"Why look at early and late arrivals one group at a time instead of giving one overall correlation?"},"l2-sensitive-q06":{"college":"In the scatterplot, what does it mean for accuracy among late arrivals to be nearly random?","simple":"In the scatterplot, what does it mean that scores for late arrivals look almost random?"},"l2-sensitive-q07":{"college":"Which result belongs to the early-arrival pattern in Johnson and Newport's data?","simple":"Which result is part of what Johnson and Newport found for people who arrived young?"},"l2-sensitive-q08":{"college":"Why does the sensitive-period interpretation consider both the early slope and the later scatter?","simple":"Why does the sensitive-period idea care about both the early downward trend and the later spread of scores?"},"l2-sensitive-q09":{"college":"Under the exercise hypothesis, what should successful early L1 acquisition protect?","simple":"The exercise hypothesis says using language early keeps something in shape. What should learning a first language early protect?"},"l2-sensitive-q10":{"college":"Older learners acquired an L1 early yet show lower L2 attainment. Which hypothesis is more directly consistent with this?","simple":"Older learners had a first language early in life but still end up worse at a second language. Which idea fits this better?"},"l2-sensitive-q11":{"college":"A learner can state an English grammatical rule correctly but struggles to apply it in spontaneous speech. Which distinction does this illustrate?","simple":"A learner can explain an English rule correctly but has trouble using it when talking freely. What difference does this show?"},"l2-sensitive-q12":{"college":"Untimed GJT scores correlate strongly with years of formal L2 instruction. Why is this significant?","simple":"Scores on the untimed GJT (grammar judgment test) go up strongly with years of second-language classes. Why does that matter?"},"l2-sensitive-q13":{"college":"At comparable arrival ages, why might Spanish and English learners differ from Chinese or Korean and English learners?","simple":"Why might Spanish speakers learning English do differently from Chinese or Korean speakers learning English, even when they arrived at the same age?"},"l2-sensitive-q14":{"college":"Late L2 learners handle questions relatively well, even though children acquire questions late in L1. Which assumption is weakened?","simple":"Late learners do fairly well with questions, even though children learn questions late in their first language. Which idea does this weaken?"},"l2-sensitive-q15":{"college":"Why might English determiners be particularly difficult for a learner whose L1 has no comparable system?","simple":"Why might English words like the and a be extra hard for someone whose first language has nothing like them?"},"l2-sensitive-q16":{"college":"A different L1 group shows a different distribution of AOA and attainment. What must a general maturational explanation also account for?","simple":"A group with a different first language shows a different link between AOA (age of arrival) and final skill. What else must a brain-growth explanation explain?"},"l2-sensitive-q17":{"college":"Why is age of arrival not automatically a pure index of maturational capacity?","simple":"Why isn't the age someone arrived a clean measure of how ready their brain was to learn?"},"l2-sensitive-q18":{"college":"An untimed judgment test and spontaneous conversation produce different relationships with age. Which interpretation is most productive?","simple":"An untimed grammar test and free conversation show different links with age. What is the most useful way to read this?"},"l2-sensitive-q19":{"college":"Which summary is best supported by the research on sensitive periods in L2 acquisition?","simple":"Which summary best fits the research on time windows for learning a second language?"},"l2-sensitive-q20":{"college":"How does difficulty with a late L2 after an early L1 complicate a simple interpretation of the delayed-L1 evidence?","simple":"People who had a first language early still struggle with a second language learned late. How does that change a simple reading of the late-first-language cases?"}}};
